WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.870
- Good evening, everybody.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:10.640
I am very delighted to see you all

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.370
for joining for this exciting event.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.130
I'm so glad to see so many of you

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:16.950
joining us tonight on Zoom.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.210
We wish we could have been in person.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.520
I'm here at the Yale Quantum Institute

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.680
on this very empty auditorium

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.253
but soon enough we'll have you over.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.020
So for those who don't know
me, my name is Florian Carle.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:32.720
I am the manager of the
Yale Quantum Institute

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.720
and the curator of the series
of non-technical talks,

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.470
co-sponsor by the Franke Program

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.730
for Science and the Humanities.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:42.880
The goal of these event
is to bring a new rigor

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.170
on quantum physics and science in general

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:48.070
by having experts cast a new look

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:51.230
on an often overlooked
aspect of scientific work.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.040
This is our 11th event

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.610
and this month mark the four
year anniversary of the series

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.733
that started in 2017.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:00.800
During this four year,

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.870
we explore the variation of topics

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.860
from music to quantum
physics, math and crochet,

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:09.520
specs exploration and board
games, biology and cloning.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.450
And tonight, we have the chance

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.750
to welcome the acclaimed
author and poet, Ben Okri,

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.583
for a special evening on
quantum physics and fiction.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:21.510
Let me introduce the speaker for tonight.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:24.600
So Ben Okri was born in Mina, Nigeria.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.640
His childhood was divided between Nigeria,

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:31.860
where he saw firsthand the
consequences of war and London.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.430
He won the Booker Prize in
1991 for "The Famished Roads"

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:40.470
and his novel, "The Astonishing the Gods",

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:45.470
was selected as BBC's 100
novel that shaped a world.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:51.360
His work has been translated
into more than 25 languages

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.150
and he also write plays and screenplay.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.580
He's a fellow of the Royal
Society of Literature

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.800
and has been awarded the
prestigious order of British Empire

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.930
as well as numerous international prices

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:03.443
and honorary doctorates.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.060
He's tonight for...

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.731
He's presenting his newest
book, "Prayer For The Living",

00:02:09.731 --> 00:02:12.160
which was released earlier
this month in the US.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.600
I do have the UK version.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:18.830
The US version has the cover
that you have behind me.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:20.520
It's a collection of short story

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.500
that includes "Alternative
Realities Are True",

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:26.560
which he's gonna read tonight.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.710
It's a strange investigation
of a murder in London

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.070
and you'll see very quickly

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:35.300
that it follows the strange
pattern of quantum physics.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.520
So this event's gonna
be split in two parts.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:38.990
Ben will read the story

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:41.830
and we'll have a slideshow
that goes along with it.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:46.130
And then after the story,
we'll have a Q and A.

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:48.110
And without further
ado, please welcome Ben

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:50.623
and feel free to start whenever you want.

00:02:52.646 --> 00:02:53.975
- Thanks, Florian.

00:02:53.975 --> 00:02:58.975
It's a real pleasure to be there and here.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.630
It's almost in itself, a quantum situation

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:10.593
that one is in two
places at the same time.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:17.820
And tonight I'll be reading
"Alternative Relatives Are True"

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:24.370
which is set in London on various verses

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:28.773
or should I say universes, multiverses?

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:33.170
I must also add that it's a
real pleasure that you're here

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:35.350
and thank you very much.

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.800
Be great in the flesh,
but when all this is over,

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.200
let's hope we can manage it.

00:03:42.497 --> 00:03:45.117
"Alternative Realities Are True".

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:47.920
It begins with a quotation.

00:03:49.877 --> 00:03:53.523
"How wonderful that we
have met with a paradox.

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:58.827
Now we have some hope of making progress,"

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:00.823
Neos Bowl.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:09.453
He woke one morning
into a strange universe.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:14.940
Everything in his one
bedroom flat in Kensal Rise

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.150
was exactly the same

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:19.240
but everything was also
slightly different.

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:21.873
He was not sure how.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:26.560
There was a message for him
on the answering machine

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.000
which he listened to with the irritation

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.563
the acute heat brought him.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:33.420
He hadn't been able to sleep.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.380
He'd been puzzling over his latest case.

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:39.500
With each new piece of information,

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.193
he was finding it more troubling.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.510
The message was from the office,

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:47.560
delivered in a verbal encryption,

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:49.993
which he simultaneously deciphered.

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:54.883
The dead man had been seen
in the cafe at the canal.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:00.620
He looked through the
crack in his curtains,

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:01.570
it was nearly dawn.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:03.830
In his garden,

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:06.180
the black birds whistled
their morning chorals.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.010
Usually, when he heard them,

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:11.603
he knew that sleep was over for him.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:16.320
This morning, there
was something different

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:17.353
about their tone.

00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:21.730
Detective Draper got out of bed

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:24.810
and slipped on his dressing gown

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.860
and went to his study to review the case.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.453
On the face of it, the case was simple.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.620
There had been a murder
near him in Kensal Rise

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:38.770
but the body of the
victim had not been found.

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.500
There was no actual proof of murder

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:44.223
but there was knowledge of it.

00:05:45.869 --> 00:05:47.770
The young overwrought Muslim woman

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:51.170
in the blue hair dress,
who gave her name as Anna

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:53.283
had come into the local police station.

00:05:54.601 --> 00:05:56.130
She had broken down and said

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:58.370
a man she knew had been murdered.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:05:59.870
She gave his name.

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:02.400
When asked how she knew
he had been murdered,

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.420
her eyes widened and
she threw up her hands.

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:08.027
She knew, she said.

00:06:08.027 --> 00:06:10.100
"Who was the murdered man?

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:11.780
Who was he to her?"

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:12.683
She was asked.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:15.223
But she would not say.

00:06:16.127 --> 00:06:17.650
"Who was the killer?"

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:18.940
She was asked.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:22.233
At this, she panicked and
fled from the station.

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:25.850
A few inquiries led them to her address

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:28.083
and a watch was placed on the building.

00:06:30.165 --> 00:06:31.890
A man by the name of Barrett,

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:35.523
the assistant of a famous artist
went missing that same day.

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:39.343
He had been known to
frequent the woman's flat.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:41.453
This was a month ago.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:44.663
Barrett had still not been found.

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:51.670
Detective Draper, who
believed more than anything,

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:55.703
that intuition was superior
to intellectual deduction,

00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:59.380
opposing himself to the theatrical methods

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:00.920
of Sherlock Holmes,

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:04.390
which had so captivated the
police force, Scotland Yard

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:05.950
and the general public,

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:07.270
knew that he was dealing with a case

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:11.080
that amounted to more than
the appearance of the facts.

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:13.400
He believed that he was indeed...

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:15.830
He believed that it was indeed essential

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:17.660
to study a case thoroughly

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:21.720
to acquaint oneself with
the locale of the crime,

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:25.230
to handle the items of
the principle players.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:28.260
In short, to furnish
the senses and instinct

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:31.840
with all the available data
and live in facts possible

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.110
and then like a fisherman

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:36.090
who must commit himself to the sea,

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:38.340
he must leave all those facts behind

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:42.483
and trust to the mysterious
dictates of intuition.

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:47.030
He had developed a faculty
of intuition as a young man

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:49.240
when he trained himself to be able to tell

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:51.170
on which side a coin would fall

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:54.790
just by following the
clearest whisper in his head.

00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:58.230
By slow degrees, applying
this method to phone calls,

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:00.930
knocks on doors, letters
that he had received,

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:02.690
guessing their contents
and who had sent them

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:05.530
before opening them for verification.

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:08.140
He had come to develop
this mysterious faculty

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:09.903
to a formidable degree.

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:15.310
Detective Draper never
spoke about this to anybody

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:19.253
knowing full well, he would
be a laughing stock if he did.

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:22.120
He masters secret technique

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:25.680
behind a tremendous amount
of paperwork and planning

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:27.623
and detailed analysis.

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:31.070
To all accounts, from the outside,

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:35.510
he was an eminently practical
detective who did his research

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:37.920
and left nothing to chance.

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:42.350
In truth, he believed in the
fertility of chance itself,

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:44.400
which he did not regard as chance at all.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:49.010
He believed that all things
in the universe were linked

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:52.523
and that every fact was
related to every other.

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:57.800
From his armchair, he solved
his cases by indirection.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.770
He was the best in the force.

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:02.963
His reputation unmatched.

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:07.430
That morning in his study,

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:09.623
he went over the case methodically.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:13.490
He realized that there was something wrong

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:14.730
with the facts of the case.

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:18.590
Some slight alteration of the world.

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:20.040
For example,

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:22.630
as soon as they put a watch
on the Muslim woman's house,

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:26.393
certain other facts of the case shifted.

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:28.340
This was puzzling.

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:33.190
The watch reported that
through the window,

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:34.920
he had seen Anna's neighbor,

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:39.023
a man named Jorg, cutting
up a large chicken.

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:43.040
The moment he noticed
the chicken being cut up,

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:45.423
the lights in Anna's room had come on.

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:50.050
Here was where it became baffling.

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:55.050
The watch swore that he saw
your both cutting up the chicken

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:59.093
and going into Anna's
room at the same time.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:04.403
The watch was a sober man
of unimpeachable reputation.

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:10.390
There were several
other curious anomalies.

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:12.380
Another taken at random,

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:14.130
was that when Jorg was seen

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:17.230
cutting up a large chicken in his room,

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:21.410
the reported victim of the
murder was seen by witnesses

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:24.893
in a cafe on the canal, a
few miles down the road.

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:29.830
Those who saw him said
that he looked well,

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:31.420
if a bit anxious.

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:33.570
And that he was reading
a book at the time.

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:35.480
Most of the witnesses

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:38.623
could not recall the title of the book.

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:43.743
Detective Draper did not
think this at all unusual.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.730
Most people he found did not notice

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:49.883
what was right under their noses.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:52.740
The inattentiveness of people

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:55.083
was a constant source of amusement to him.

00:10:56.260 --> 00:11:00.050
He had come to rely on their
failure to notice things.

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:04.440
His best deductions came
from working round the bends

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:05.893
of this failure.

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:09.973
Questioning one of the witnesses,

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:13.800
he was able, through indirection,

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:15.803
to work out the title of the book.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:18.080
To any other detectives,

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:21.830
such a detail as a title of
a book a victim was reading

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:24.920
would be insignificant
but to detective Draper,

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:28.053
nothing in the universe was insignificant.

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:31.500
The witness, remembered in fact,

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:34.173
that the victim was reading a cat.

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:38.263
What do you mean he was reading a cat?

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:40.710
He was reading a cat.

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:44.793
Did he have an actual cat in his hand?

00:11:46.030 --> 00:11:46.883
Both hands.

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:49.663
What color was the cat?

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:53.083
It did not have a color.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:55.533
Did it have a name?

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:58.730
The last question had
come to detective Draper

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:00.510
purely by intuition.

00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:03.233
It had simply dropped
into his mind like that.

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:05.470
But behind the intuition,

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:08.260
hovering like the ghost of knowledge,

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:09.093
was the understanding

00:12:09.093 --> 00:12:12.710
that some people see words as real things

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:16.170
and that they are incapable
of the abstract faculty

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:18.800
of reading, transferring
everything they read

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:19.640
that has an image

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:22.083
straight away into its
physical equivalent.

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:24.300
This was one of those anomalous facts

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:27.533
that the detective
stored away in his mind.

00:12:29.090 --> 00:12:30.593
Yes, it did have a name.

00:12:31.490 --> 00:12:32.323
What was it?

00:12:33.195 --> 00:12:34.950
It was a very strange name.

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:36.930
Before you tell me what it was,

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:38.053
tell me how you knew.

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:40.850
It's strange, but I just did.

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:42.670
I looked at the cat he was reading

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:45.070
and its name popped into
my head just like that.

00:12:46.777 --> 00:12:49.377
"There is no need to tell
me the name of the cat,"

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:53.103
the detective said nonchalantly.

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:55.990
Why not?

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:57.963
I know it already, but thank you.

00:13:00.042 --> 00:13:02.650
The detective rose to signify
the end of the interview,

00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:05.390
leaving the witness quite baffled.

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:08.230
Detective Draper, upset minded,

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:10.580
the moment he was consumed by a clue,

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:12.780
called several bookshops.

00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:13.820
In a short time,

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:15.853
he found out what he wanted to know.

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:19.290
A copy of the book had
been purchased that day,

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:21.120
quite early in the morning

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:24.413
from Any Amount Of Books
on Charing Cross Road.

00:13:25.355 --> 00:13:28.423
It was the only copy of the
book purchased that morning.

00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:33.590
A check confirmed that the
book had been bought by a Jorg,

00:13:33.590 --> 00:13:35.763
as the detective had suspected.

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:39.600
Another call to the local police station

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:41.083
confirmed something else.

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:45.253
That the victim had not been seen since.

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:49.180
As it stood, there were two sightings.

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:53.760
One was of the suspected
killer in two places

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:55.820
at the same time.

00:13:55.820 --> 00:14:00.480
The other was of the victim
after he had gone missing

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:02.113
and was presumed dead.

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:05.350
For the rest of the day,

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:07.543
contradictory reports kept pouring in.

00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:11.963
A warrant for the arrest of
Jorg led to his detention.

00:14:12.940 --> 00:14:15.830
The large chicken he
had been seen cutting up

00:14:15.830 --> 00:14:18.383
was brought in as evidence.

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:20.600
But that same evening,

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:25.600
Jorg, who was in police custody,
was seen in Anna's room.

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:31.227
He was shouting at her at
one point called her a whore.

00:14:31.227 --> 00:14:33.360
"I have killed that lover of yours

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:37.007
and there's nothing anyone
can do about it," he said.

00:14:37.007 --> 00:14:38.470
"What have you done to him?"

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:39.610
She cried.

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:40.580
I have hidden him

00:14:40.580 --> 00:14:42.913
where no one will ever
be able to find him.

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:45.840
She was heard wailing

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:49.460
and Jorg was seen departing from the room.

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:51.490
The watch was unable to do anything

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:53.840
because he had conclusive proof

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:57.753
that the murder suspect
was in police custody.

00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:02.963
He had no legal right to
arrest the same person twice.

00:15:05.230 --> 00:15:07.520
Detective Draper considered
the arrest of Jorg

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:10.660
to have been an act of sublime stupidity

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:11.893
by the local police.

00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:16.293
The detective dropped
in at the local station.

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:20.320
The sergeant on duty, a fixed set man,

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:22.700
was reading "May Gray".

00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:24.033
His name was Pillock.

00:15:24.914 --> 00:15:28.637
And he had reputation for blue sickness.

00:15:28.637 --> 00:15:30.577
"The way I see it, detective," he said,

00:15:30.577 --> 00:15:33.040
"is that we know a
crime has been committed

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:34.780
but we have no evidence.

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:37.393
We suspect it's a murder,
but there is no body.

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:41.890
And he could be a potential terrorist.

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:44.440
We have to keep him locked
up for the public good."

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:48.283
The detective contemplated
Sergeant Pillock.

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:51.560
There was no use arguing with him.

00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:53.343
Explanation was equally futile.

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:58.780
He would have to add the heavy
footedness of the sergeant

00:15:58.780 --> 00:15:59.993
into the equation.

00:16:01.637 --> 00:16:05.663
"But do you see that the problem
multiplies," Draper said.

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:09.073
I see that it simplifies.

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:11.693
Any new evidence?

00:16:12.980 --> 00:16:15.123
Only CCTV footage on a bus.

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:17.013
What number?

00:16:18.170 --> 00:16:20.567
Six, is that significant?

00:16:20.567 --> 00:16:23.140
"Everything is significant," Draper said.

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:24.490
What did the footage yield?

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:27.760
Something puzzling.

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:28.593
What?

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:31.860
The suspect made several
trips in one direction

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:34.460
with black plastic bags.

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:36.430
He appeared to be moving house.

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:38.103
We never saw him get off.

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:40.463
Anything else?

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:43.390
Yes, but it doesn't make sense.

00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:44.670
Tell me.

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:46.470
It stretches credibility.

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:47.303
Let's have it.

00:16:48.430 --> 00:16:49.280
On one of the cameras,

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:52.273
we caught an image of the
bus just as it exploded.

00:16:53.420 --> 00:16:55.030
Did it explode?

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:57.170
That's just it, it didn't.

00:16:57.170 --> 00:16:59.410
We believe the CCTV system has been hacked

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:01.883
and false footage has been planted there.

00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:07.320
Unless there is an
equally radical solution

00:17:07.320 --> 00:17:09.320
but I fear we may be too late.

00:17:09.320 --> 00:17:11.250
We have to act fast.

00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:12.403
What shall we do?

00:17:14.103 --> 00:17:15.780
The detective did a rapid calculation.

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:18.333
He felt himself uncircled by time.

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:20.360
The first thing he did

00:17:20.360 --> 00:17:24.340
was order that the watch
be pulled off Anna's house.

00:17:24.340 --> 00:17:27.000
Then he suggested that
the suspect to be released

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:28.300
and not followed.

00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:29.620
Not followed?

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:31.040
No.

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:32.263
But he must be followed.

00:17:33.250 --> 00:17:35.330
It will only make things worse.

00:17:35.330 --> 00:17:36.163
How?

00:17:37.527 --> 00:17:42.527
"We are being invaded by one
of many possible futures,"

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:43.783
Draper said.

00:17:44.762 --> 00:17:45.680
What was that?

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:46.803
Nothing, forget it.

00:17:47.841 --> 00:17:49.720
Are you on something?

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:52.673
No, I'm sober as a surgeon on duty.

00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:55.170
Well then you need to fill me in.

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:59.193
Detective Draper knew he could
not explain the intuition

00:17:59.193 --> 00:18:00.893
that was filtering through to him.

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:05.383
Weirdly, he sought the most
innocuous expression of it.

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:08.053
He softened his voice as he spoke.

00:18:10.400 --> 00:18:14.003
Everything we do influences
what the suspect does.

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:16.713
We are multiplying the problem.

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:21.540
Before, there was one murderer.

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:23.583
Now, there are three.

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:26.150
Three?

00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:27.093
Which three?

00:18:28.167 --> 00:18:31.690
"Free," the detective corrected himself.

00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:33.223
I meant he should be free.

00:18:35.260 --> 00:18:36.093
Two.

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:42.630
Detective Draper knew
there were only two ways

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:43.883
to resolve the case.

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:47.650
One was to prevent the murder.

00:18:47.650 --> 00:18:49.973
The other was to uncover it.

00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:52.440
All the evidence suggested

00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:55.320
it was too late to prevent the murder.

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:56.850
It had happened.

00:18:56.850 --> 00:19:00.070
The past had now been lost.

00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:01.760
If the victim had been apprehended

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:04.090
when he was seen reading the cat,

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:06.210
things might've been different.

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:07.563
That moment was gone.

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:10.883
It was lost because no one noticed

00:19:10.883 --> 00:19:14.230
that the past lurks in the present.

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:18.843
Not just past events,
but the past as present.

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:23.400
Detective Draper liked to walk

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:26.423
after he had been immersed
in facts too long.

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:30.310
When he had spent all
day reading documents,

00:19:30.310 --> 00:19:33.160
studying evidence, listening to witnesses,

00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:36.880
when he was saturated
with too much information,

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:40.020
he would walk in a zigzag
path through side streets

00:19:40.020 --> 00:19:40.853
to his home.

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:45.430
Anyone seeing him would think
he was wondering aimlessly

00:19:47.240 --> 00:19:50.693
but his walks always followed
the pattern of his thoughts.

00:19:52.400 --> 00:19:55.680
He had always known
that the indirect route

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:57.903
led more quickly to his destination.

00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:03.100
The direct routes were often
bogged down in interruptions,

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:06.173
conversations and crowded streets.

00:20:08.130 --> 00:20:11.000
That evening, after a
long spell at the station,

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:11.833
he left late.

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:15.700
His thoughts had gone off
the map of indirection.

00:20:16.580 --> 00:20:18.940
He had taken so many side streets

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:21.550
that he did not recognize where he was.

00:20:21.550 --> 00:20:22.393
He didn't mind.

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:27.090
His best intuitions came
while he was walking

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:29.510
and while waking.

00:20:29.510 --> 00:20:32.960
Or rather, his best
intuitions came while waking

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:34.573
and while walking.

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:38.313
In an odd way, he seemed
to be doing both now.

00:20:39.290 --> 00:20:43.590
He seemed to be waking from
the sleep of his thoughts.

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:44.453
Where was he?

00:20:45.870 --> 00:20:46.773
He looked around.

00:20:48.010 --> 00:20:49.270
He had gone off the main road

00:20:49.270 --> 00:20:51.333
that led to his flat in Kensal Rise.

00:20:53.440 --> 00:20:57.760
The street where he now stood
looked decidedly strange.

00:20:57.760 --> 00:20:59.560
He searched for the street name

00:20:59.560 --> 00:21:01.953
and found that he did
not even recognize that.

00:21:03.100 --> 00:21:05.200
He walked on 'til he came to a barbershop.

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:08.240
There were no customers.

00:21:08.240 --> 00:21:10.270
When he went in, he startled a barber

00:21:10.270 --> 00:21:11.770
who was reading the newspaper.

00:21:12.627 --> 00:21:14.900
"Can you please tell me where I am?"

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:18.137
The barber stared at
him with his mouth open.

00:21:18.137 --> 00:21:19.193
"Is anything wrong?

00:21:20.530 --> 00:21:23.683
You came in here about five
minutes ago," the barber said.

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:26.967
"And you asked the very same question."

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:32.520
Detective Draper looked around
at the clock on the wall

00:21:32.520 --> 00:21:34.500
with its Roman numerals.

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:36.413
It was 6:35 p.m.

00:21:38.167 --> 00:21:42.243
"So I did, so I did,"
detective Draper murmured.

00:21:43.090 --> 00:21:44.463
And he hurried out.

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:48.370
He went back the way he had come.

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:52.800
A glance told him that the
barber had come out of his shop

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:53.850
and was watching him.

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:55.940
He walked faster.

00:21:55.940 --> 00:21:57.870
Aware that the faster he walked,

00:21:57.870 --> 00:22:00.793
the faster walked all the
events connected to him.

00:22:02.290 --> 00:22:04.973
If he sped up, things would speed up too.

00:22:06.030 --> 00:22:09.683
The web of connected events
responded to his every deed.

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:13.580
Suddenly, in his mind,
he could see the lines

00:22:13.580 --> 00:22:16.080
that linked him to the victim

00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:18.930
and the murderer and the woman.

00:22:18.930 --> 00:22:21.040
Except that these lines had multiplied

00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:22.690
in ways he could only guess that.

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:26.200
He was walking aimlessly now

00:22:27.090 --> 00:22:29.053
following the vectors of his thoughts.

00:22:30.100 --> 00:22:32.610
He conjectured that if he had arrived

00:22:32.610 --> 00:22:35.560
at this strange place by getting lost,

00:22:35.560 --> 00:22:37.990
then only by getting lost again

00:22:37.990 --> 00:22:39.923
could he return to a familiar world.

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:44.250
He was thinking fast and walking fast

00:22:44.250 --> 00:22:46.793
as if something unknown were stalking him.

00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:50.443
The Lincoln lines moved
faster in his mind.

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:53.053
Then he suddenly stopped.

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:56.000
In the middle of all that wandering,

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:59.290
a clear thought distilled in pristine

00:22:59.290 --> 00:23:01.510
dropped into his mind.

00:23:01.510 --> 00:23:03.360
Why hadn't he thought of that before?

00:23:05.330 --> 00:23:07.680
There was now only one
way to solve the murder.

00:23:09.043 --> 00:23:10.799
The past was close to him.

00:23:10.799 --> 00:23:13.050
There was only one path left.

00:23:13.050 --> 00:23:16.853
He had to arrive at a future
place before the murderer did.

00:23:17.910 --> 00:23:21.823
He had to outwit him in
all the vectors of time.

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:27.880
At this point, the murderer
had a time advantage over him.

00:23:28.990 --> 00:23:31.120
He had to overturn that advantage.

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:34.713
He had to anticipate the
murderer in the future.

00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:37.863
He walked home much more slowly.

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:41.440
He noticed the Kensal estates

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:45.023
and the silver birches
and the uneven pavement.

00:23:46.410 --> 00:23:49.453
It did not surprise him that
he now knew where he was.

00:23:50.920 --> 00:23:53.703
He walked slowly and breathed evenly.

00:23:54.970 --> 00:23:58.303
As he turned into his street,
someone brushed past him.

00:23:59.668 --> 00:24:01.710
The detective caught a confident smile

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:03.710
on the face of the man who adjusted him.

00:24:05.210 --> 00:24:07.080
He was at his front gate when he realized

00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:10.523
that the smile belonged
to the murder suspect.

00:24:11.710 --> 00:24:14.183
But when he turned to
look, the man was gone.

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:18.820
There was an envelope
pinned to his front door.

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:20.760
Inside the envelope was a message

00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:24.840
composed of letters cut out
of newspapers and magazines.

00:24:24.840 --> 00:24:25.733
The message read,

00:24:26.610 --> 00:24:29.483
all alternative realities are true.

00:24:32.953 --> 00:24:35.670
The detective let himself into his flat,

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:37.580
shut the door behind him

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:41.683
and poured himself a glass
of Argentinian Altamira.

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:44.780
He sat down at a table near a window

00:24:44.780 --> 00:24:46.133
to contemplate the letter.

00:24:47.750 --> 00:24:52.313
After a while, he brought out
a one book of Escher drawings.

00:24:53.530 --> 00:24:55.520
Leaving the door in his mind open,

00:24:55.520 --> 00:24:56.970
he contemplated the drawings.

00:24:58.030 --> 00:25:01.223
But in the other rooms in
his mind, he was thinking.

00:25:03.630 --> 00:25:07.250
In his research, he had discovered

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:10.170
that the murder suspect
had an unusual interest

00:25:10.170 --> 00:25:12.103
in a branch of speculative physics.

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:15.550
In one room in his mind,

00:25:15.550 --> 00:25:17.530
he realized that the solution to the crime

00:25:17.530 --> 00:25:19.950
was not to be found in the real world,

00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:24.223
but in an unreal space,
a speculative realm.

00:25:25.540 --> 00:25:27.050
In another room,

00:25:27.050 --> 00:25:29.943
he pondered the message he had received.

00:25:31.770 --> 00:25:35.163
All alternative realities are true.

00:25:37.060 --> 00:25:39.480
He contemplated the
ramifications of the message

00:25:39.480 --> 00:25:41.490
while leafing through the drawings.

00:25:41.490 --> 00:25:46.490
Calmly, he began to chart out
the clear lines of the case.

00:25:46.610 --> 00:25:49.520
Soon, he came to a dead end.

00:25:49.520 --> 00:25:52.293
He paused and put down the book.

00:25:54.610 --> 00:25:58.480
He knew that all dead ends are an illusion

00:25:59.470 --> 00:26:01.573
designed to bring motion to a halt.

00:26:03.120 --> 00:26:06.380
To those who can see beyond the illusion,

00:26:06.380 --> 00:26:10.083
dead ends are portals into
unknown possibilities.

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:16.440
He paid more attention to dead
ends than to open highways.

00:26:18.160 --> 00:26:21.453
He knew now that a leap
of faith was required.

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:25.473
Then he remembered the
cat that both men had.

00:26:26.440 --> 00:26:30.233
Then he remembered the cat that
was both seen and not seen.

00:26:31.330 --> 00:26:33.830
He realized he was going
to have to work backward.

00:26:35.800 --> 00:26:38.080
He went to sleep that
night with the feeling

00:26:38.080 --> 00:26:39.980
that he had at least stopped

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:42.163
the multiplication of the problem.

00:26:44.076 --> 00:26:45.730
Part three.

00:26:45.730 --> 00:26:47.030
The next morning,

00:26:47.030 --> 00:26:50.550
an envelope was posted
through his letterbox.

00:26:50.550 --> 00:26:52.250
Before he opened it,

00:26:52.250 --> 00:26:54.300
he knew that he would find letters

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:56.360
cut out of newspapers and magazines

00:26:56.360 --> 00:26:58.803
composing on elusive message.

00:26:59.660 --> 00:27:02.490
The envelope contained a
single sheet of white paper

00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:06.463
with the words, the world is still here.

00:27:09.130 --> 00:27:11.260
He had a shower, got dressed,

00:27:11.260 --> 00:27:15.253
regarded his face in the mirror.

00:27:15.253 --> 00:27:18.790
The mirror had a slight warp that morning.

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:22.903
He was not sure that the
world was still there.

00:27:24.280 --> 00:27:28.350
Outside, the weather had changed.

00:27:28.350 --> 00:27:30.700
The sun shone intermittently

00:27:32.110 --> 00:27:34.130
through the dark masses of clouds.

00:27:34.130 --> 00:27:38.283
Cool wind with a deceptively
icy core, blew from the south.

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:41.580
He went down the side
streets towards the cafe

00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:45.723
where the victim, Barrett,
had been seen reading a cat.

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:51.510
The cafe was run by a long-haired Italian

00:27:51.510 --> 00:27:53.640
who combined obsequiousness

00:27:53.640 --> 00:27:56.733
with a mildly intrusive personal charm.

00:27:57.940 --> 00:27:59.910
Sitting in the same seat

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:02.190
where the victim had
been seen reading a cat,

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:06.193
detective Draper saw the
canal beyond the balcony.

00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:10.103
There were boats mod on both sides.

00:28:11.330 --> 00:28:14.103
The detective drunk his hot water slowly.

00:28:15.250 --> 00:28:18.290
He had given up drinking coffee years ago

00:28:18.290 --> 00:28:23.290
and now settled for the digestive
refinements of hot water.

00:28:23.990 --> 00:28:27.873
Outside, birds were
wheeling over the canal.

00:28:29.730 --> 00:28:32.803
While sipping, he had a singular thought.

00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:37.020
He knew that entertaining
it would create realities

00:28:37.020 --> 00:28:39.480
that would only blur the case further.

00:28:39.480 --> 00:28:42.793
So he pushed the thought
to a corner of his mind.

00:28:43.810 --> 00:28:45.693
He caught a taxi back to the office.

00:28:46.700 --> 00:28:47.910
To test his theory,

00:28:47.910 --> 00:28:51.320
he had his contact at the
Evening Standard plan to report

00:28:51.320 --> 00:28:55.000
that the police were draining
an obscure canal in Richmond

00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:57.023
in their search for the missing body.

00:28:58.260 --> 00:29:01.480
To lens this deception the
veneer of plausibility,

00:29:01.480 --> 00:29:06.053
he had two amateur frogmen
busily explore the canal.

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:09.080
They were to pretend to pull something out

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:10.423
of its shallow depths.

00:29:11.297 --> 00:29:13.850
"What's the idea behind
this flagrant waste

00:29:13.850 --> 00:29:18.100
of public funds on sham
frogmen in a remote canal?"

00:29:18.100 --> 00:29:22.227
Asked his superintendent, who
had summoned him to lunch.

00:29:22.227 --> 00:29:24.230
"You are aware that I
have gone out on a limb

00:29:24.230 --> 00:29:29.230
for you too many times and I've
never let you down, have I?"

00:29:30.920 --> 00:29:34.133
Detective Draper replied,
calmly buttering in his roll.

00:29:35.407 --> 00:29:36.690
"No you haven't, I grant that,

00:29:36.690 --> 00:29:39.600
but there are many people after your job.

00:29:39.600 --> 00:29:41.440
They will use anything to get at you.

00:29:41.440 --> 00:29:44.687
God knows you have enough
disadvantages as it is."

00:29:44.687 --> 00:29:46.120
"Do I?"

00:29:46.120 --> 00:29:49.100
Detective Draper said while
the glimmer of a new thought

00:29:49.100 --> 00:29:51.830
filtered through a door in his mind.

00:29:51.830 --> 00:29:54.870
You know you do, you're
not an Oxbridge man.

00:29:54.870 --> 00:29:56.310
Neither are you.

00:29:56.310 --> 00:29:59.290
You are black.

00:29:59.290 --> 00:30:02.290
I wasn't going to say
that, but you thought it.

00:30:02.290 --> 00:30:07.290
Look, we run the most color
blind department in the country.

00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:10.200
You took the words right out of my mouth

00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:12.010
but I'm the only one that is...

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:15.190
We have to face the
realities of the world.

00:30:16.890 --> 00:30:18.690
Detective Draper suddenly stiffened.

00:30:19.667 --> 00:30:21.510
He held his head at an angle.

00:30:21.510 --> 00:30:23.927
A new thought had come through.

00:30:23.927 --> 00:30:24.760
"Do we?"

00:30:24.760 --> 00:30:26.193
He said absentmindedly.

00:30:27.297 --> 00:30:29.470
"Yes, we do," replied the superintendent,

00:30:29.470 --> 00:30:31.673
puzzled by Draper's behavior.

00:30:33.071 --> 00:30:36.623
Have you had this
conversation with me before?

00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:39.610
That's what I was getting to.

00:30:39.610 --> 00:30:41.370
About an hour ago?

00:30:41.370 --> 00:30:42.583
Yes, why?

00:30:43.940 --> 00:30:47.170
We don't have any time,
we have to stop it now.

00:30:47.170 --> 00:30:48.003
Stop what?

00:30:49.290 --> 00:30:52.340
There will be another
murder unless we act fast.

00:30:52.340 --> 00:30:53.173
Murder?

00:30:53.173 --> 00:30:54.470
Of who?

00:30:54.470 --> 00:30:56.780
The woman, she'll be murdered

00:30:56.780 --> 00:30:58.710
and there'll be no evidence of it.

00:30:58.710 --> 00:31:00.260
Why not?

00:31:00.260 --> 00:31:03.610
Because she would have
been murdered yesterday.

00:31:03.610 --> 00:31:05.093
Detective Draper stood up.

00:31:06.187 --> 00:31:07.860
"You're not making sense, man,"

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:09.327
the superintendent exploded.

00:31:09.327 --> 00:31:11.457
"Sit down and explain yourself."

00:31:12.800 --> 00:31:15.320
I really must dash, sir.

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:18.070
I'll have a full explanation
for you tomorrow afternoon,

00:31:18.070 --> 00:31:19.063
if all goes well.

00:31:21.399 --> 00:31:24.820
"But tomorrow is Saturday,"
the superintendent said

00:31:24.820 --> 00:31:27.240
to the departing figure of the detective.

00:31:27.240 --> 00:31:29.663
Yorkshire are playing
Middlesex at the oval.

00:31:30.757 --> 00:31:33.070
"I know," Draper said

00:31:33.070 --> 00:31:35.801
before he disappeared through the door.

00:31:35.801 --> 00:31:38.247
"And the score will be 17 to one."

00:31:38.247 --> 00:31:39.530
"What?"

00:31:39.530 --> 00:31:40.780
Cried the superintendent.

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:44.723
Detective Draper was
already out on the street.

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:47.573
Part four.

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:52.490
From the Kensal Rise police station,

00:31:52.490 --> 00:31:55.720
detective Draper
requisitioned three frogmen.

00:31:56.710 --> 00:31:58.100
Early on Saturday morning,

00:31:58.100 --> 00:32:01.040
he had them dive into the
canal at Little Venice,

00:32:01.040 --> 00:32:02.173
right near the cafe.

00:32:03.210 --> 00:32:05.083
The area was cordoned off.

00:32:06.640 --> 00:32:09.620
The frogmen in their dark wetsuits

00:32:09.620 --> 00:32:11.613
began their mysterious exploration.

00:32:12.950 --> 00:32:14.370
There was a unit of police there

00:32:14.370 --> 00:32:16.143
with an unmistakable police van.

00:32:17.820 --> 00:32:20.193
Detective Draper watched from a distance.

00:32:21.290 --> 00:32:23.630
If his intuition was correct,

00:32:23.630 --> 00:32:26.343
in the next hour a number
of things would happen.

00:32:27.260 --> 00:32:29.453
Their precise order interested him.

00:32:30.930 --> 00:32:34.600
He knew he was in the realm
of a speculative space now

00:32:34.600 --> 00:32:37.760
where actually seen what was sought

00:32:37.760 --> 00:32:40.203
made his existence real.

00:32:41.460 --> 00:32:44.193
The time, the opportunity were limited.

00:32:45.330 --> 00:32:47.793
Things were in mutant formation.

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:51.660
Events were hovering between two realms,

00:32:51.660 --> 00:32:53.923
between being and non being.

00:32:54.890 --> 00:32:57.593
From here on, the
operation will be delicate.

00:32:59.060 --> 00:33:03.130
The frogmen in the obscure
canal at Richmond found nothing

00:33:03.130 --> 00:33:04.513
as was the intention.

00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:08.510
But the story planted in
the "Evening Standard"

00:33:08.510 --> 00:33:12.573
had this desired effect, as far
as the detective could tell.

00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:15.683
He kept looking at his watch.

00:33:16.540 --> 00:33:19.240
The frogmen here, at
the Little Venice canal,

00:33:19.240 --> 00:33:21.163
took turns in going in.

00:33:22.383 --> 00:33:24.150
One of them would stand
on the edge of the canal

00:33:24.150 --> 00:33:26.243
and then flip over backwards.

00:33:27.720 --> 00:33:30.853
He would route around below
for awhile and then emerge.

00:33:32.280 --> 00:33:35.130
For an hour now, they had been exploring

00:33:35.130 --> 00:33:36.230
and had found nothing.

00:33:37.780 --> 00:33:40.513
The cricket game had began at the Oval.

00:33:41.480 --> 00:33:44.482
The heat was beginning to rise.

00:33:44.482 --> 00:33:47.540
It was a bright day and
people had begun to stop

00:33:47.540 --> 00:33:51.143
to watch the strange
proceedings on the canal.

00:33:52.010 --> 00:33:54.270
This too had been factored

00:33:54.270 --> 00:33:58.293
into detective Draper's
complex calculation of events.

00:33:59.220 --> 00:34:03.500
Only by the frogmen dive in,
verifying and bringing up,

00:34:03.500 --> 00:34:08.263
could there be anything to
dive for, verify and bring up.

00:34:10.044 --> 00:34:11.393
The detective knew this.

00:34:12.340 --> 00:34:13.770
Something will be found

00:34:13.770 --> 00:34:16.830
only because something is looked for.

00:34:16.830 --> 00:34:19.133
The looking creates the finding.

00:34:20.900 --> 00:34:22.250
In just the same way,

00:34:22.250 --> 00:34:26.480
only the presence of
onlookers created the event.

00:34:26.480 --> 00:34:28.163
They onlookers were essential.

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:31.030
The detective had surmised

00:34:31.030 --> 00:34:34.690
that what was needed
was a waiting of events,

00:34:34.690 --> 00:34:38.340
a way in of events toward
a more dense reality

00:34:38.340 --> 00:34:42.250
to counter balance the
ambiguous reality between worlds

00:34:42.250 --> 00:34:45.823
which had so far bedeviled
the investigation.

00:34:46.660 --> 00:34:50.713
He intuited that all worlds
had to come together here.

00:34:51.680 --> 00:34:54.930
That was why even before
he had full permission,

00:34:54.930 --> 00:34:58.540
he'd arranged for the frogmen
to make a public exhibition

00:34:58.540 --> 00:35:03.053
of excavating the muddy
depths of the canal.

00:35:04.420 --> 00:35:06.250
The ruse had worked.

00:35:06.250 --> 00:35:08.530
The item planted in the "Evening Standard"

00:35:08.530 --> 00:35:11.210
had caught the lively
interest of Londoners.

00:35:11.210 --> 00:35:13.163
And soon everyone was talking about it.

00:35:14.350 --> 00:35:17.780
The crowd around the canal,
observing the frogmen,

00:35:17.780 --> 00:35:19.370
grew in number.

00:35:19.370 --> 00:35:23.393
The trap sprung had yielded
multidimensional fruits.

00:35:24.310 --> 00:35:28.280
An off-duty policemen had
spotted the suspected murderer

00:35:28.280 --> 00:35:30.180
near the Little Venice canal

00:35:30.180 --> 00:35:32.980
and had reported the matter
to the local police station.

00:35:33.880 --> 00:35:36.140
The station chief had relayed the message

00:35:36.140 --> 00:35:37.283
to detective Draper.

00:35:38.140 --> 00:35:39.850
With a nonchalant expression,

00:35:39.850 --> 00:35:43.103
he continued to observe
the frogmen at work.

00:35:44.860 --> 00:35:48.243
Twice, the frogmen claimed
to have found nothing.

00:35:50.490 --> 00:35:53.410
They had said it was just
mud and rotten bicycles

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:55.533
and broken puddles down there.

00:35:56.760 --> 00:35:57.593
The detective,

00:35:57.593 --> 00:35:59.860
with his keen awareness
of the power of numbers,

00:35:59.860 --> 00:36:03.470
said twice does not make
anything conclusive.

00:36:03.470 --> 00:36:07.010
The canal could seal secrets
which must be exposed.

00:36:07.010 --> 00:36:08.140
My career depends on it.

00:36:08.140 --> 00:36:09.523
Maybe even my life.

00:36:11.570 --> 00:36:13.923
He sent the frogmen down a third time.

00:36:14.800 --> 00:36:18.160
He suggested that they look
not so much in the deeper parts

00:36:18.160 --> 00:36:21.617
but in the parts no one
would think of looking.

00:36:21.617 --> 00:36:23.080
"What part is that, sir?"

00:36:23.080 --> 00:36:24.280
One of the frogman said.

00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:27.363
You will know when you find it.

00:36:28.480 --> 00:36:30.513
Disgruntled, they returned to work.

00:36:31.780 --> 00:36:33.350
They flipped over backwards

00:36:33.350 --> 00:36:36.073
and disappeared beneath
the muddied surface.

00:36:37.120 --> 00:36:40.253
Detective Draper, aware of
the proximity of the murderer,

00:36:41.521 --> 00:36:44.543
knew that it was now a race against time.

00:36:45.560 --> 00:36:47.770
The frogmen had to find something

00:36:47.770 --> 00:36:49.883
before the murderer found him.

00:36:51.150 --> 00:36:52.463
He remained calm.

00:36:53.360 --> 00:36:55.863
He ordered a cup of hot
water from the cafe.

00:36:56.910 --> 00:37:00.450
The cafe manager, Vin Insusient, Italian,

00:37:00.450 --> 00:37:01.750
brought the water himself.

00:37:02.870 --> 00:37:05.303
His de-shoveled locks
fell across his face.

00:37:06.230 --> 00:37:10.200
The Italian handed the
detective the cup of hot water

00:37:10.200 --> 00:37:12.057
and held onto the pot.

00:37:12.057 --> 00:37:14.090
"So what is going on, sir?"

00:37:14.090 --> 00:37:15.373
The cafe manager asked.

00:37:16.550 --> 00:37:19.200
Detective Draper turns to look at him.

00:37:19.200 --> 00:37:22.970
The Italian had the eyes of an insomniac.

00:37:22.970 --> 00:37:25.070
It's best if you don't know.

00:37:25.070 --> 00:37:26.900
We don't want you entangled

00:37:26.900 --> 00:37:28.800
in the more fatal webs of the problem.

00:37:30.161 --> 00:37:31.840
I might be able to help.

00:37:31.840 --> 00:37:33.070
How?

00:37:33.070 --> 00:37:34.600
I hear things.

00:37:34.600 --> 00:37:35.923
People tell me things.

00:37:37.020 --> 00:37:38.200
I'm sure that's true.

00:37:38.200 --> 00:37:41.277
I'll contact you if your help is required.

00:37:41.277 --> 00:37:43.787
"Anytime," said the Italian.

00:37:43.787 --> 00:37:45.787
"It would be an honor to be of service."

00:37:47.470 --> 00:37:48.790
The cafe manager stood there

00:37:48.790 --> 00:37:53.063
watching the first frogman
emerge from the mucky canal.

00:37:54.391 --> 00:37:57.180
The detective turns to
him with a furrowed brow

00:37:57.180 --> 00:37:59.370
and the Italian immediately understood

00:37:59.370 --> 00:38:01.600
that his presence had become redundant.

00:38:01.600 --> 00:38:03.730
He filled the detectives cup

00:38:03.730 --> 00:38:05.503
and retreated into his cafe.

00:38:07.600 --> 00:38:11.143
Detective Draper felt the
murderer moving closer.

00:38:12.320 --> 00:38:15.253
But he sipped his hot
water with perfect calm.

00:38:16.647 --> 00:38:18.360
"It's not the number of encounters

00:38:18.360 --> 00:38:20.483
in the numberless realms that counts,

00:38:21.350 --> 00:38:25.220
it is the world in which
the encounter is fixed,"

00:38:25.220 --> 00:38:27.173
thought the detective, grimly.

00:38:28.500 --> 00:38:30.280
A flurry among the fragments told him

00:38:30.280 --> 00:38:33.180
that the nature of the
game had shifted at last.

00:38:33.180 --> 00:38:35.270
He drunk water was left of his hot water

00:38:35.270 --> 00:38:36.343
and went toward them.

00:38:37.640 --> 00:38:39.540
One of the frogmen had pulled out,

00:38:39.540 --> 00:38:43.660
from the depths of the
canal, a black plastic bag.

00:38:43.660 --> 00:38:47.033
Dripping with muck, it
was placed on the ground.

00:38:48.250 --> 00:38:50.000
The frogman hosed it down

00:38:51.070 --> 00:38:53.410
and then the detective had it brought

00:38:53.410 --> 00:38:54.560
to the back of the van.

00:38:55.580 --> 00:38:57.530
A casual glance at his form

00:38:57.530 --> 00:38:59.223
told him what he needed to know.

00:39:00.530 --> 00:39:02.723
There was no need to expose the contents.

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:06.733
The first stage of the
duel had been fought.

00:39:07.920 --> 00:39:09.010
It might've been difficult

00:39:09.010 --> 00:39:12.100
to fix the reality of
this black dustbin bag

00:39:12.100 --> 00:39:13.880
but now that it was fixed,

00:39:13.880 --> 00:39:15.463
the rest was sure to be found.

00:39:16.520 --> 00:39:18.300
It was at that moment,

00:39:18.300 --> 00:39:21.150
perhaps delayed by the variable factors

00:39:21.150 --> 00:39:23.320
in the calculations of time,

00:39:23.320 --> 00:39:26.143
that a shadow moved into site.

00:39:27.410 --> 00:39:29.650
Jorg fired two shots.

00:39:29.650 --> 00:39:31.670
One pierced the metal of the white van,

00:39:31.670 --> 00:39:34.823
the other grazed detective
Draper's shoulder.

00:39:36.060 --> 00:39:38.070
Then Jorg was wrestled to the ground

00:39:38.070 --> 00:39:39.980
by the plainclothes policemen

00:39:39.980 --> 00:39:44.023
who had been drinking cups
of innocuous tea in the cafe.

00:39:45.260 --> 00:39:46.700
Before the detective fell,

00:39:46.700 --> 00:39:47.757
he was heard to cry out,

00:39:47.757 --> 00:39:51.257
"on no account are the frogmen
to seize the exploration."

00:39:52.340 --> 00:39:55.320
He passed out for only a few moments.

00:39:55.320 --> 00:39:59.550
An act of will wrenched him
back into consciousness.

00:39:59.550 --> 00:40:02.400
Detective Draper allowed the paramedics

00:40:02.400 --> 00:40:04.000
to bandage his shoulder

00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:06.543
while he surveyed the scene before him.

00:40:07.700 --> 00:40:09.430
The frogmen had persisted

00:40:09.430 --> 00:40:13.670
and eventually they had dredged
up three black bin liners

00:40:13.670 --> 00:40:15.463
from diverse parts of the canal.

00:40:16.310 --> 00:40:18.580
They were hosed down and
kept in the back of the van

00:40:18.580 --> 00:40:20.733
where the bullet had pierced.

00:40:21.930 --> 00:40:25.410
The detective had no need
to inspect their contents.

00:40:25.410 --> 00:40:26.713
He knew what they were.

00:40:28.470 --> 00:40:32.110
The afternoon sun played
on the water of the canal

00:40:32.110 --> 00:40:35.243
making its surface shine like gun metal.

00:40:36.220 --> 00:40:37.610
The light came through the trees

00:40:37.610 --> 00:40:39.623
on both sides of the metal fences.

00:40:40.780 --> 00:40:45.280
Detective Draper could see
the church through the leaves.

00:40:45.280 --> 00:40:46.363
He felt tired.

00:40:47.710 --> 00:40:50.270
He made a signal to one of the men.

00:40:50.270 --> 00:40:51.103
They bore him into a waiting car,

00:40:51.103 --> 00:40:53.300
but instead of being taken to the hospital

00:40:53.300 --> 00:40:54.740
for further treatment,

00:40:54.740 --> 00:40:56.963
he insisted on being taken home.

00:40:59.130 --> 00:40:59.963
Section five.

00:41:02.300 --> 00:41:04.923
The next morning, with his arm in a sling,

00:41:05.760 --> 00:41:08.550
detective Draper was
at the police station.

00:41:08.550 --> 00:41:10.400
Out of respect for the part

00:41:10.400 --> 00:41:11.740
he had played in the investigation,

00:41:11.740 --> 00:41:13.760
he was allowed the first conference

00:41:13.760 --> 00:41:15.260
with the man who had shot him.

00:41:16.280 --> 00:41:17.933
They were in a soundproof room.

00:41:19.193 --> 00:41:22.030
The detective sat on a chair at the table

00:41:22.030 --> 00:41:24.137
and the murderer sat opposite.

00:41:24.137 --> 00:41:26.273
"Something puzzles me," said Jorg.

00:41:27.324 --> 00:41:29.360
There was nothing repentant
about his expression.

00:41:29.360 --> 00:41:31.893
He stared at the detective intensely.

00:41:33.900 --> 00:41:36.813
You want to know how I knew where to look.

00:41:37.990 --> 00:41:39.620
Jorg smiled.

00:41:39.620 --> 00:41:40.740
It was a thin smile

00:41:40.740 --> 00:41:43.193
sustained by something
resembling a twitch.

00:41:44.220 --> 00:41:45.553
Then he nodded faintly.

00:41:47.377 --> 00:41:51.367
"My question to you," said Draper.

00:41:51.367 --> 00:41:52.200
"Is why?"

00:41:54.030 --> 00:41:54.863
Why what?

00:41:56.300 --> 00:41:57.540
They were still dueling.

00:41:58.738 --> 00:42:00.530
It was not over yet.

00:42:00.530 --> 00:42:04.037
There was still the chance
of further multiplications.

00:42:05.820 --> 00:42:06.870
He had to be careful.

00:42:08.250 --> 00:42:09.300
Why did you kill him?

00:42:10.700 --> 00:42:13.700
Are you sure it is why and not when?

00:42:13.700 --> 00:42:14.920
I know when.

00:42:14.920 --> 00:42:15.753
You do?

00:42:17.580 --> 00:42:19.880
You're not going to trap
me that easily again.

00:42:20.890 --> 00:42:22.740
I have no idea what you mean.

00:42:23.637 --> 00:42:27.580
"Of course you do," said Draper.

00:42:27.580 --> 00:42:29.333
All alternative realities are true.

00:42:30.387 --> 00:42:32.247
"I see," said Jorg.

00:42:32.247 --> 00:42:33.233
"You got the clue.

00:42:34.750 --> 00:42:36.850
It was designed to throw me off the scent.

00:42:38.380 --> 00:42:41.013
You went against your on
psychology," said Jorg.

00:42:41.857 --> 00:42:44.950
"Like you, I have many psychologies,"

00:42:44.950 --> 00:42:46.550
the detective said leaning back.

00:42:47.490 --> 00:42:48.740
But why did you kill him?

00:42:49.730 --> 00:42:50.830
He was after my woman.

00:42:51.840 --> 00:42:53.740
She was not your woman.

00:42:53.740 --> 00:42:56.010
She told the police that
you were just her neighbor

00:42:56.010 --> 00:42:58.110
and that you misunderstood her friendship.

00:42:59.160 --> 00:43:01.983
She was a whore and he deserved to die.

00:43:03.510 --> 00:43:05.063
He was not even one of us.

00:43:06.450 --> 00:43:08.650
You killed him out of jealousy.

00:43:08.650 --> 00:43:10.683
Not jealousy, revenge.

00:43:12.490 --> 00:43:13.860
The detective stood up.

00:43:13.860 --> 00:43:15.033
He felt weary.

00:43:16.510 --> 00:43:20.160
He was at the door when Jorg
fired the last question at him.

00:43:20.160 --> 00:43:21.123
How did you know?

00:43:23.576 --> 00:43:24.580
The detective at that moment

00:43:24.580 --> 00:43:26.433
felt a throb of pain in his shoulder.

00:43:27.767 --> 00:43:31.301
"The world is still here," he replied.

00:43:31.301 --> 00:43:32.134
He went out.

00:43:33.710 --> 00:43:34.793
Section six.

00:43:36.410 --> 00:43:40.010
Three hours later, he was
sitting with the superintendent

00:43:40.910 --> 00:43:42.513
in the Scotland Yard canteen.

00:43:43.550 --> 00:43:46.053
They had window seats
looking out over the river.

00:43:47.630 --> 00:43:49.463
The facts don't make sense.

00:43:50.550 --> 00:43:52.420
Can you make sense of them for me

00:43:52.420 --> 00:43:54.070
so I can explain it to the board?

00:43:55.710 --> 00:43:57.453
I don't recommend an explanation.

00:43:58.360 --> 00:43:59.253
What then?

00:44:00.730 --> 00:44:02.090
Leave them with the facts.

00:44:02.090 --> 00:44:04.660
Sometimes people need to
be brought face-to-face

00:44:04.660 --> 00:44:08.393
with the incomprehensibility of the world.

00:44:10.030 --> 00:44:11.680
The superintendent stared at him.

00:44:12.807 --> 00:44:16.097
"Now you know why you will
never make department head,"

00:44:17.820 --> 00:44:19.270
he said.

00:44:19.270 --> 00:44:20.653
It was never my ambition.

00:44:22.040 --> 00:44:23.503
You have ambitions?

00:44:25.077 --> 00:44:28.510
"I have ambitions that
perhaps you won't understand,"

00:44:28.510 --> 00:44:32.200
said the detective sipping his hot water.

00:44:32.200 --> 00:44:35.603
Besides, there are things
better than ambition.

00:44:37.290 --> 00:44:39.833
How can you stand to drink
that tasteless stuff?

00:44:41.377 --> 00:44:43.810
"It's not tasteless at all," Draper said.

00:44:45.557 --> 00:44:49.225
"It restores me to the fundamental
simplicity of the world."

00:44:49.225 --> 00:44:51.940
The world seems to be anything but simple.

00:44:51.940 --> 00:44:54.690
Take this case for example,
what on earth was going on?

00:44:57.190 --> 00:45:00.130
It wouldn't make sense if I explained it.

00:45:00.130 --> 00:45:00.963
Try me.

00:45:02.377 --> 00:45:04.203
It was a multiverse murder.

00:45:05.500 --> 00:45:06.340
What on earth is that?

00:45:06.340 --> 00:45:07.653
Speak plain English, man.

00:45:08.748 --> 00:45:12.063
It was a murder that
happened in many universes.

00:45:13.070 --> 00:45:14.013
You must be mad.

00:45:15.610 --> 00:45:18.463
In one universe, the
murder hadn't happened yet.

00:45:19.460 --> 00:45:20.853
In another, it had.

00:45:22.010 --> 00:45:25.640
Each time we apprehended
him, we multiplied...

00:45:25.640 --> 00:45:26.900
Okay, that's enough of that.

00:45:26.900 --> 00:45:28.220
If I hear anymore,

00:45:28.220 --> 00:45:30.560
I might start to have
crackpot ideas myself.

00:45:30.560 --> 00:45:32.623
Just telling me this, why the canal?

00:45:36.231 --> 00:45:37.870
I realized it when I learned

00:45:37.870 --> 00:45:42.130
the victim was seen in
the cafe reading a cat.

00:45:42.130 --> 00:45:43.253
Reading a cat?

00:45:45.520 --> 00:45:49.350
The policeman who saw him was dyslexic.

00:45:49.350 --> 00:45:50.683
His condition was extreme.

00:45:52.290 --> 00:45:54.363
He saw virtually what he read.

00:45:55.542 --> 00:45:57.903
About 1% of dyslexics have this syndrome.

00:45:59.270 --> 00:46:01.060
What was the cat he was reading?

00:46:01.060 --> 00:46:02.633
It was a book.

00:46:03.480 --> 00:46:05.130
The book gave me the second clue.

00:46:06.020 --> 00:46:08.300
The rest unraveled itself.

00:46:08.300 --> 00:46:09.560
Out with it, man.

00:46:09.560 --> 00:46:11.000
You're tying me up in riddles.

00:46:11.000 --> 00:46:12.043
What was the book?

00:46:13.031 --> 00:46:15.063
"Schrodinger's Cat".

00:46:15.063 --> 00:46:16.063
Schrodinger's what?

00:46:18.050 --> 00:46:19.757
He had bought the book that morning

00:46:19.757 --> 00:46:22.480
and was seen reading it in the cafe

00:46:22.480 --> 00:46:23.830
after he had been murdered.

00:46:24.830 --> 00:46:28.050
Unless we are dealing with operations,

00:46:28.050 --> 00:46:29.700
there could be only one solution.

00:46:30.920 --> 00:46:33.873
The superintendent looked
red faced and exasperated.

00:46:35.170 --> 00:46:37.900
I had to somehow get all the universes

00:46:37.900 --> 00:46:41.930
in which the murder was
multiplying to converge.

00:46:41.930 --> 00:46:46.527
I had to create what physicist
called an event convergence.

00:46:46.527 --> 00:46:47.940
"What on earth is that?"

00:46:47.940 --> 00:46:49.760
Bellowed the superintendent,

00:46:49.760 --> 00:46:52.733
by now a swollen image of himself.

00:46:53.820 --> 00:46:58.050
It is the one event, the one
thing that will fix time.

00:46:58.050 --> 00:46:59.990
In quantum mechanics, it is conjecture

00:46:59.990 --> 00:47:02.880
that the universe only
comes into existence

00:47:02.880 --> 00:47:04.203
when we perceive it.

00:47:05.180 --> 00:47:08.000
I surmise that perhaps
the body of the victim

00:47:08.000 --> 00:47:11.560
would only be found when we find it.

00:47:11.560 --> 00:47:14.763
The crime did not exist
'til the body was found.

00:47:16.899 --> 00:47:19.160
And the body was in the bin bags?

00:47:19.160 --> 00:47:19.993
Exactly.

00:47:21.150 --> 00:47:22.173
How did you know?

00:47:23.900 --> 00:47:26.630
One of the watchers
outside the woman's house

00:47:26.630 --> 00:47:30.350
had seen the murder
suspect cutting a chicken.

00:47:30.350 --> 00:47:31.543
It was a chicken.

00:47:32.710 --> 00:47:36.733
It was a chicken because the
watcher had seen a chicken.

00:47:38.050 --> 00:47:39.830
Do you mean to say...

00:47:39.830 --> 00:47:40.663
Yes.

00:47:41.920 --> 00:47:44.830
The superintendent
paused for a long moment.

00:47:44.830 --> 00:47:47.593
But why was he trying to kill you?

00:47:49.620 --> 00:47:52.223
He wasn't really, we have to understand

00:47:52.223 --> 00:47:55.713
that this man was taking a cosmic gamble.

00:47:57.000 --> 00:48:00.160
He never really had any
intention of killing anybody.

00:48:00.160 --> 00:48:02.700
He was overcome with rage, with jealousy,

00:48:02.700 --> 00:48:05.040
with a kind of love madness.

00:48:05.040 --> 00:48:06.310
In that state it occurred to him

00:48:06.310 --> 00:48:09.640
that maybe if he killed
the man in one universe,

00:48:09.640 --> 00:48:12.240
the man would still be alive in another.

00:48:12.240 --> 00:48:13.210
Somewhere along the line,

00:48:13.210 --> 00:48:15.003
he forgot what university was in.

00:48:16.136 --> 00:48:18.410
In short, he lost his reason.

00:48:18.410 --> 00:48:19.843
You mean he went mad?

00:48:21.256 --> 00:48:24.010
It depends on what you think madness is.

00:48:24.010 --> 00:48:24.843
Continue.

00:48:26.170 --> 00:48:27.700
In one of those universes,

00:48:27.700 --> 00:48:31.520
it's occurred to him
that if he could stop me

00:48:31.520 --> 00:48:36.200
before I could find the body,
then he would never be caught

00:48:36.200 --> 00:48:38.650
and the murder will, to all accounts,

00:48:38.650 --> 00:48:40.393
be forever speculative.

00:48:42.040 --> 00:48:43.990
Why wasn't I told all this at the time?

00:48:44.990 --> 00:48:47.073
You were at your cricket match, sir.

00:48:48.120 --> 00:48:49.943
A terrible game, we were mauled.

00:48:50.860 --> 00:48:52.690
Sorry to hear that, sir.

00:48:52.690 --> 00:48:54.540
Still, you could have sent a message.

00:48:55.385 --> 00:48:57.230
It was a delicate matter.

00:48:57.230 --> 00:48:58.230
What does that mean?

00:48:59.210 --> 00:49:00.260
I had to arrive at the body

00:49:00.260 --> 00:49:02.143
before the bullets arrived in me.

00:49:04.140 --> 00:49:05.373
There was a long pause.

00:49:07.347 --> 00:49:09.620
"Detective Draper, you look terrible,"

00:49:09.620 --> 00:49:11.117
the superintendent said.

00:49:11.117 --> 00:49:13.217
"When was the last time you had some sun?"

00:49:14.240 --> 00:49:15.620
Can't remember, sir.

00:49:15.620 --> 00:49:17.090
Get some sun.

00:49:17.090 --> 00:49:20.380
I want that shoulder
working when you get back.

00:49:20.380 --> 00:49:22.163
Take a holiday, detective Draper.

00:49:23.297 --> 00:49:27.767
"Thank you, sir," the detective
said with a wry smile.

00:49:27.767 --> 00:49:31.857
"I think I might while
the world is still here."

00:49:34.433 --> 00:49:35.810
- That was a fantastic story.

00:49:35.810 --> 00:49:39.143
Thank you so much for taking
the time to read it to us.

00:49:40.534 --> 00:49:41.374
- It was a pleasure.

00:49:41.374 --> 00:49:42.207
It's a pleasure.

00:49:42.207 --> 00:49:44.273
Much more fun than I feared it would be.

00:49:45.617 --> 00:49:48.950
- We're having a bunch
of people in the Q and A,

00:49:48.950 --> 00:49:50.740
setting pressing question and things,

00:49:50.740 --> 00:49:52.940
but the first thing that I want to ask you

00:49:52.940 --> 00:49:56.380
is that there's very few literary works

00:49:56.380 --> 00:49:58.220
about quantum physics.

00:49:58.220 --> 00:49:59.530
The one that comes in mind

00:49:59.530 --> 00:50:02.300
is the Michael Fray's play, "Copenhagen"

00:50:02.300 --> 00:50:05.510
that staged the discussion
between Neil Bors,

00:50:05.510 --> 00:50:08.720
which you used as an
epigraph for your story

00:50:08.720 --> 00:50:11.670
and Heisenberg and his wife, Norland.

00:50:11.670 --> 00:50:15.180
But murder in quantum physics
is kind of a unique mix.

00:50:15.180 --> 00:50:16.840
Like, can you tell us a little bit

00:50:16.840 --> 00:50:20.433
why you choose quantum
physics to stage this murder

00:50:20.433 --> 00:50:22.303
and how did the story come together?

00:50:22.303 --> 00:50:24.000
Or like, what was your process?

00:50:25.013 --> 00:50:27.140
- Well, it's very hard to talk of process

00:50:27.140 --> 00:50:29.113
with a story like this.

00:50:30.973 --> 00:50:33.190
I don't know if I should tell
you how the story came about,

00:50:33.190 --> 00:50:35.843
it might actually spoil the
wonderful mystery of it.

00:50:35.843 --> 00:50:38.200
Let's just say very simply,

00:50:38.200 --> 00:50:43.200
that I was put in a position
where I had to write a story,

00:50:43.763 --> 00:50:44.833
a detective story.

00:50:45.932 --> 00:50:48.190
And I've read so many detective stories

00:50:48.190 --> 00:50:50.211
and I thought to myself,

00:50:50.211 --> 00:50:52.860
I really don't know how I can add anything

00:50:52.860 --> 00:50:57.150
to this really famous genre.

00:50:57.150 --> 00:50:59.510
And at the same time, for some time,

00:50:59.510 --> 00:51:00.700
in fact, for a long time,

00:51:00.700 --> 00:51:05.677
I've been having a very secret
interest in quantum physics

00:51:05.677 --> 00:51:06.840
and quantum mechanics

00:51:06.840 --> 00:51:08.580
and I've been reading
around the edges of it

00:51:08.580 --> 00:51:11.040
for all sorts of reasons,

00:51:11.040 --> 00:51:14.170
partly because of
fascination with this field

00:51:14.170 --> 00:51:15.330
and this subject

00:51:15.330 --> 00:51:20.330
because it's one of the
few fields in science

00:51:20.540 --> 00:51:22.770
that for the first time

00:51:22.770 --> 00:51:26.140
actually begins to
undermine the certainties

00:51:26.140 --> 00:51:27.563
that we have about reality.

00:51:28.520 --> 00:51:30.810
And also one of the fields in science

00:51:30.810 --> 00:51:33.360
that for the first time begins to hint

00:51:33.360 --> 00:51:38.360
at kind of a spectral unseen
and fascinating dimension

00:51:39.160 --> 00:51:42.993
to existence and to the
world as we think we know it.

00:51:44.020 --> 00:51:48.010
It's in fact, it's an era that
really troubles the surface

00:51:48.010 --> 00:51:49.060
of our set certainties.

00:51:49.060 --> 00:51:51.510
And I found that really,
really, really magnetic.

00:51:52.830 --> 00:51:56.820
So I thought to myself,
wouldn't it be wonderful

00:51:56.820 --> 00:52:01.820
if I brought a great secret
love with a public problem?

00:52:05.880 --> 00:52:08.300
I have to write a detective story

00:52:08.300 --> 00:52:09.133
and wouldn't it be great

00:52:09.133 --> 00:52:10.600
if I brought the two things together?

00:52:10.600 --> 00:52:13.890
So I conceive of the
idea of detective Draper

00:52:13.890 --> 00:52:18.010
and the heart of the case

00:52:18.010 --> 00:52:22.900
would be an issue
concerning quantum physics,

00:52:22.900 --> 00:52:24.500
quantum mechanics.

00:52:24.500 --> 00:52:26.470
The uncertainty of the world,

00:52:26.470 --> 00:52:31.470
the way in which that which
we look at changes by looking

00:52:31.770 --> 00:52:36.329
and the idea of superpositions
and various things like that.

00:52:36.329 --> 00:52:38.280
And at the same time, I
wanted to write the story

00:52:38.280 --> 00:52:42.040
in such a way that the
story itself was symbolic

00:52:42.040 --> 00:52:43.250
of the quantum universe

00:52:43.250 --> 00:52:46.761
or at least a way in which things act

00:52:46.761 --> 00:52:49.430
as far as we know on that level.

00:52:49.430 --> 00:52:52.509
And I was just fascinated by
this abstruse, this obscure,

00:52:52.509 --> 00:52:57.140
this challenge, this difficult problem.

00:52:57.140 --> 00:52:59.930
And it was that more than anything.

00:52:59.930 --> 00:53:02.110
But also I think the
thing that fascinated me

00:53:02.110 --> 00:53:04.567
was it was the relationship
between writing itself

00:53:04.567 --> 00:53:05.720
and the quantum world.

00:53:05.720 --> 00:53:10.640
I find there's a great resonance
between the two things.

00:53:10.640 --> 00:53:14.340
So you have to understand, Florian,

00:53:14.340 --> 00:53:18.311
that as a child,

00:53:18.311 --> 00:53:21.070
one of the first things I
wanted to be was a scientist.

00:53:21.070 --> 00:53:22.700
I wanted to be a physicist.

00:53:22.700 --> 00:53:23.820
If I'd known, when I was a kid,

00:53:23.820 --> 00:53:26.563
that there was such a thing
as speculative physics,

00:53:27.890 --> 00:53:29.110
you wouldn't be talking to me now.

00:53:29.110 --> 00:53:31.090
I'd be where you are.

00:53:31.090 --> 00:53:32.258
Where you are.

00:53:32.258 --> 00:53:36.000
- (laughs) That's wonderful.

00:53:36.000 --> 00:53:38.510
It's interesting because
I had the opposite things.

00:53:38.510 --> 00:53:40.360
I was doing theater before

00:53:40.360 --> 00:53:43.110
and then switched midway
to become a scientist.

00:53:43.110 --> 00:53:44.650
So maybe that's what we are.

00:53:44.650 --> 00:53:46.250
We are just opposites.

00:53:46.250 --> 00:53:48.857
We switched at the same time (laughs).

00:53:50.430 --> 00:53:52.580
I have another question for you.

00:53:52.580 --> 00:53:56.440
You use real places to anchor your story.

00:53:56.440 --> 00:53:58.530
And we talked about,

00:53:58.530 --> 00:54:00.770
when I proposed to you to read the story,

00:54:00.770 --> 00:54:01.840
you're like, "oh! it would be great

00:54:01.840 --> 00:54:04.640
to have some way to like
not have the audience

00:54:04.640 --> 00:54:06.123
just watch me read."

00:54:06.123 --> 00:54:07.130
And I was like, what can I do?

00:54:07.130 --> 00:54:09.850
And I started looking at maps of London

00:54:09.850 --> 00:54:10.740
and I starting to see

00:54:10.740 --> 00:54:13.180
that I could actually
pinpoint all the details,

00:54:13.180 --> 00:54:15.460
like all the places that
you mentioned in the book,

00:54:15.460 --> 00:54:18.970
the bookshop, the Kenal,
the police station,

00:54:18.970 --> 00:54:21.277
all the streets are real and they exist.

00:54:21.277 --> 00:54:24.500
And like, can you tell us why
you picked like a real place?

00:54:24.500 --> 00:54:25.590
Is it because you were worried

00:54:25.590 --> 00:54:30.420
that if you have like a weird
location plus quantum physics

00:54:30.420 --> 00:54:31.930
that would be too much for the reader?

00:54:31.930 --> 00:54:32.763
Or you were like,

00:54:32.763 --> 00:54:34.450
"oh! This is the place
that I like, that I know

00:54:34.450 --> 00:54:35.809
and I can create something

00:54:35.809 --> 00:54:40.047
that can have multiple varieties on this."

00:54:41.080 --> 00:54:42.530
- All of the above, actually.

00:54:48.318 --> 00:54:50.880
I think one of the great
secret laws of writing,

00:54:52.620 --> 00:54:57.620
which is that the real is
underpinned by the unreal

00:54:58.647 --> 00:55:01.333
and the unreal is supported by the real.

00:55:02.550 --> 00:55:06.590
These two things kind of
work together invisibly

00:55:06.590 --> 00:55:08.930
in just the same way
that the quantum world

00:55:08.930 --> 00:55:12.575
and what we perceive of as
the world of certainties

00:55:12.575 --> 00:55:15.040
operate at the same time.

00:55:15.040 --> 00:55:19.940
One is concealed or as it were
exists deep inside the other.

00:55:19.940 --> 00:55:21.786
So one needs the reality.

00:55:21.786 --> 00:55:26.786
I find that to investigate
on reality, you need reality

00:55:26.860 --> 00:55:29.570
to really look deeply into a problem.

00:55:29.570 --> 00:55:33.120
The more truthful the place
is, the more real the place is,

00:55:33.120 --> 00:55:34.400
the easier it is for you

00:55:34.400 --> 00:55:38.463
to actually plunge into your exploration.

00:55:40.560 --> 00:55:42.320
- Yeah, that's wonderful.

00:55:42.320 --> 00:55:44.620
I'm gonna start reading
through the question

00:55:44.620 --> 00:55:48.100
that the audience is putting
on the Q and A panel.

00:55:48.100 --> 00:55:49.640
We have a first question

00:55:49.640 --> 00:55:51.830
which is maybe more common recommended

00:55:51.830 --> 00:55:52.950
from Michelle Doberay,

00:55:52.950 --> 00:55:56.550
who's one of the quantum
physicists that we have at YQI.

00:55:56.550 --> 00:55:59.470
He says fantastic quantum story

00:55:59.470 --> 00:56:02.300
and something is puzzling him.

00:56:02.300 --> 00:56:03.260
Is the story built

00:56:03.260 --> 00:56:05.350
around the picture of
a fragment in the Kenal

00:56:05.350 --> 00:56:06.850
or is it the opposite?

00:56:06.850 --> 00:56:08.700
And the reason why I'm putting this

00:56:08.700 --> 00:56:10.170
is because it's a funny comment

00:56:10.170 --> 00:56:11.800
that Michelle is really well known.

00:56:11.800 --> 00:56:16.610
That to name all of these
quantum objects after animals.

00:56:16.610 --> 00:56:18.980
So we had, of course the cats,

00:56:18.980 --> 00:56:21.790
but there's a snail happening.

00:56:21.790 --> 00:56:24.620
There's also some squid and
so I wouldn't be worried,

00:56:24.620 --> 00:56:26.740
I wouldn't be surprised
that in the near future,

00:56:26.740 --> 00:56:30.030
one of the quantum
devices had a frog somehow

00:56:30.030 --> 00:56:31.452
linked into this.

00:56:31.452 --> 00:56:33.050
(all laughing)

00:56:33.050 --> 00:56:35.770
I will keep an eye on
that, I'll let you know

00:56:35.770 --> 00:56:37.630
and maybe you should
take some of the credit

00:56:37.630 --> 00:56:40.467
if we see a quantum computer
with a frog (indistinct).

00:56:42.563 --> 00:56:45.830
(laughs) We have another question

00:56:45.830 --> 00:56:49.260
from somebody that you
probably know, Priya Nafarajan,

00:56:49.260 --> 00:56:51.640
who's the director of the Frankie Program

00:56:51.640 --> 00:56:54.290
and she said, "I love
the layers of reality

00:56:54.290 --> 00:56:56.260
that you explore in this story.

00:56:56.260 --> 00:56:59.490
Just curious, do you believe
that narratives are stable

00:56:59.490 --> 00:57:01.740
or are you playing with
this notion as well?"

00:57:03.000 --> 00:57:04.683
- Yeah, all wonderful questions.

00:57:06.930 --> 00:57:09.133
Again, narrative,

00:57:10.810 --> 00:57:13.633
the relationship between
reality and the quantum world,

00:57:15.090 --> 00:57:18.350
narrative, while you're
reading it, the arc of it

00:57:18.350 --> 00:57:20.440
while you're emersed in
it and reading the words

00:57:20.440 --> 00:57:21.850
and following the story

00:57:21.850 --> 00:57:25.123
and the characters and
the plot, appear stable.

00:57:27.066 --> 00:57:30.770
When you finished reading
it and you've left the text,

00:57:30.770 --> 00:57:35.180
the inherent stability
narrative then begins to act

00:57:35.180 --> 00:57:39.540
on memory, mind, emotion,
nostalgia, replay

00:57:39.540 --> 00:57:40.373
and all of that.

00:57:40.373 --> 00:57:41.880
Then you go back to the text again

00:57:42.870 --> 00:57:44.673
and then something strange happens.

00:57:45.810 --> 00:57:49.752
What was fixed and familiar
and certain and definite

00:57:49.752 --> 00:57:54.683
undergoes occasionally
wobbles and wrinkles.

00:57:55.650 --> 00:57:56.793
It changes.

00:57:58.350 --> 00:58:00.220
You finished again with this new change

00:58:00.220 --> 00:58:01.980
and then you go back
and you live your life

00:58:01.980 --> 00:58:03.640
and then you come back to the text again.

00:58:03.640 --> 00:58:05.100
And the same thing has happened.

00:58:05.100 --> 00:58:07.050
So is narrative stable?

00:58:07.050 --> 00:58:07.920
Is it unstable?

00:58:07.920 --> 00:58:09.972
It's impossible to say.

00:58:09.972 --> 00:58:11.450
It's the same.

00:58:11.450 --> 00:58:12.830
This is what I'm trying to say.

00:58:12.830 --> 00:58:15.280
That the literature, that writing,

00:58:15.280 --> 00:58:17.770
shares something of the quantum universe,

00:58:17.770 --> 00:58:19.043
it really, really does.

00:58:20.870 --> 00:58:23.360
Because when you have it in front of you,

00:58:23.360 --> 00:58:25.570
you take a famous line that
like to be or not to be,

00:58:25.570 --> 00:58:26.700
you have it in front of you.

00:58:26.700 --> 00:58:31.400
It is absolutely fixed just
like this chair or this table

00:58:31.400 --> 00:58:32.300
as I'm looking at.

00:58:33.446 --> 00:58:36.701
But you come back to
it, you break it down,

00:58:36.701 --> 00:58:39.960
you look into it, you move away from it,

00:58:39.960 --> 00:58:41.110
you carry it around in your spirit,

00:58:41.110 --> 00:58:42.410
you come back to it another time

00:58:42.410 --> 00:58:44.300
and something about it has shifted,

00:58:44.300 --> 00:58:46.163
something about it has altered.

00:58:47.020 --> 00:58:50.870
And I think narrative plays and
works with our consciousness

00:58:50.870 --> 00:58:55.590
in the same way that the reality
of reality works on warps

00:58:55.590 --> 00:58:58.770
and changes and shifts
with our consciousness.

00:58:58.770 --> 00:58:59.950
I think the two things are linked.

00:58:59.950 --> 00:59:04.950
I didn't choose the quantum
universe arbitrarily

00:59:05.210 --> 00:59:06.330
in the writing of the story.

00:59:06.330 --> 00:59:08.450
It's also meant to be a parallel

00:59:08.450 --> 00:59:09.960
of the way in which literature,

00:59:09.960 --> 00:59:14.557
the way in which words,
letters, punctuation,

00:59:16.910 --> 00:59:21.570
these little quantum tools
as it were work on the mind

00:59:22.533 --> 00:59:23.663
and on consciousness.

00:59:26.680 --> 00:59:28.240
- Thank you very much.

00:59:28.240 --> 00:59:32.100
Another question I have is
that "Prayer For The Living",

00:59:32.100 --> 00:59:34.993
your new book, is a very political book.

00:59:36.973 --> 00:59:38.700
Yeah, you have the new
version, even better.

00:59:38.700 --> 00:59:39.820
Here we go.

00:59:39.820 --> 00:59:41.550
I will put in the chat.

00:59:41.550 --> 00:59:44.010
So I think you will have,
in the invitation from Zoom,

00:59:44.010 --> 00:59:44.843
you have the link.

00:59:44.843 --> 00:59:48.070
If you wish to buy the book,
you should be able to do it

00:59:48.070 --> 00:59:50.360
through different online sellers.

00:59:50.360 --> 00:59:53.100
So feel free to grab a copy of it.

00:59:53.100 --> 00:59:54.510
All the stories are really wonderful

00:59:54.510 --> 00:59:56.220
and I highly recommend this book.

00:59:56.220 --> 00:59:57.660
It's pretty good.

00:59:57.660 --> 00:59:59.152
And so-

00:59:59.152 --> 01:00:01.270
- Thank you, Florian.

01:00:01.270 --> 01:00:04.355
- It seems to be the less
subversive than for example,

01:00:04.355 --> 01:00:07.670
if we can run them the
first story, "Boko Haram",

01:00:07.670 --> 01:00:10.450
about a suicide bomber child

01:00:10.450 --> 01:00:12.610
and still in this murder mystery,

01:00:12.610 --> 01:00:14.571
which seems very operatical,

01:00:14.571 --> 01:00:18.040
you explore some racial tension
in the police departments

01:00:18.040 --> 01:00:21.050
between the detective Draper
and the superintendent.

01:00:21.050 --> 01:00:22.610
And it's a very shocking thing

01:00:22.610 --> 01:00:24.810
because there's been a lot of discussion

01:00:24.810 --> 01:00:29.320
about race and racial prejudices
and systematic racism.

01:00:29.320 --> 01:00:33.750
And even the story when the
reader is focusing on the loops

01:00:33.750 --> 01:00:35.840
and like all the clues
and all the details,

01:00:35.840 --> 01:00:37.900
you bring them back into reality

01:00:37.900 --> 01:00:40.420
and realize that even in
this alternate reality

01:00:40.420 --> 01:00:43.660
is you have the same flow and prejudices.

01:00:43.660 --> 01:00:46.780
I find it like an
incredibly effective tool,

01:00:46.780 --> 01:00:48.940
like narrative tool to
like really bring it back.

01:00:48.940 --> 01:00:53.000
Like, is it something that
you thought in events like,

01:00:53.000 --> 01:00:53.833
oh! This is a way,

01:00:53.833 --> 01:00:55.850
or did it came more organically say like,

01:00:55.850 --> 01:00:59.350
because this is what usually happens

01:00:59.350 --> 01:01:01.053
in this kind of a structure?

01:01:02.550 --> 01:01:05.520
- Yes, well, it came organically

01:01:05.520 --> 01:01:10.520
because I wanted you, first of
all, to follow this detective

01:01:12.830 --> 01:01:17.700
purely from a point of
view of the tradition.

01:01:18.900 --> 01:01:20.930
The tradition of detectives
like Sherlock Holmes,

01:01:20.930 --> 01:01:23.410
like Lupin that's
suddenly become famous now

01:01:23.410 --> 01:01:25.180
because of the Netflix series.

01:01:25.180 --> 01:01:26.720
I want you to follow, first of all,

01:01:26.720 --> 01:01:31.720
enter into a genre identification
with detective Draper

01:01:35.240 --> 01:01:40.130
and to enter this world,
enter the exploration with him

01:01:40.130 --> 01:01:42.283
just purely on that basis.

01:01:43.180 --> 01:01:45.973
On the pleasure of that tradition.

01:01:47.410 --> 01:01:48.650
But at some point,

01:01:48.650 --> 01:01:52.750
I wanted to pull the reader up short

01:01:52.750 --> 01:01:55.350
with the unstated reality.

01:01:55.350 --> 01:02:00.300
I'm fascinated by unstated
realities in reading,

01:02:00.300 --> 01:02:04.780
'cause I think we're overwhelmed
by stated realities enough

01:02:04.780 --> 01:02:08.240
to a point we stop seeing them,

01:02:08.240 --> 01:02:11.780
we stop noticing the
depth of certain problems,

01:02:11.780 --> 01:02:13.270
the depth of certain realities.

01:02:13.270 --> 01:02:14.560
We stop noticing them

01:02:14.560 --> 01:02:19.560
because we acquire kind
of object blindness

01:02:19.850 --> 01:02:22.500
from too much familiarity
and too much ease

01:02:22.500 --> 01:02:27.500
and too much superficial
saturation in a sense.

01:02:29.100 --> 01:02:32.080
And I wanted to drain
the story of all of that.

01:02:32.080 --> 01:02:33.870
I'm free to experience that fact

01:02:33.870 --> 01:02:36.550
which was there all along
towards the end of the story

01:02:36.550 --> 01:02:37.751
or in the middle of it.

01:02:37.751 --> 01:02:40.460
Just to sprinkle it
and then come back out.

01:02:40.460 --> 01:02:43.162
Because I think sometimes
when you invert things,

01:02:43.162 --> 01:02:45.933
they're stronger.

01:02:47.710 --> 01:02:49.220
It's just a new way

01:02:49.220 --> 01:02:53.927
of dealing with one of the
facts of a rich universe.

01:02:58.974 --> 01:03:00.809
- Thank you very much.

01:03:00.809 --> 01:03:01.973
- Thank you.

01:03:01.973 --> 01:03:03.730
It's a lovely tough question.

01:03:03.730 --> 01:03:04.930
- Yeah, I know (laughs).

01:03:05.890 --> 01:03:08.420
We have some more question in the chat.

01:03:08.420 --> 01:03:10.570
Somebody said that I was curious

01:03:10.570 --> 01:03:12.740
about the reference to the bus explosion

01:03:12.740 --> 01:03:17.130
and the murderer potentially
being a terrorist.

01:03:17.130 --> 01:03:20.290
Are there social political intent

01:03:20.290 --> 01:03:21.970
in introducing these elements

01:03:21.970 --> 01:03:23.640
in light of the story being written

01:03:23.640 --> 01:03:26.260
through the eyes of a police officer

01:03:26.260 --> 01:03:28.350
trying to make sense of ambiguous events

01:03:28.350 --> 01:03:30.763
enfolding in the London older class?

01:03:32.280 --> 01:03:34.170
- Yes, absolutely.

01:03:34.170 --> 01:03:39.170
I wanted these political
realities to be there

01:03:39.240 --> 01:03:42.680
within what seems to be a genre fiction

01:03:43.780 --> 01:03:48.780
that is complicated by quantum mechanics.

01:03:49.310 --> 01:03:53.070
I love the idea of having these realities.

01:03:53.070 --> 01:03:54.870
They're also in London where I live,

01:03:57.670 --> 01:04:00.003
near parts of Kensal Rise.

01:04:00.950 --> 01:04:03.550
There's been a lot of those
kinds of tensions there.

01:04:04.700 --> 01:04:09.257
I mean, the story has to
use a garden as terms,

01:04:11.750 --> 01:04:12.753
healthy roots.

01:04:14.293 --> 01:04:18.300
It's still rooted in real things

01:04:18.300 --> 01:04:22.510
that have given a more
mysterious expression.

01:04:22.510 --> 01:04:25.520
So I want it to be true
to a reality that I knew

01:04:26.970 --> 01:04:30.080
even while writing this,

01:04:30.080 --> 01:04:32.433
what seems like a piece of genre fiction.

01:04:34.810 --> 01:04:35.796
- [Florian] Thank you.

01:04:35.796 --> 01:04:37.783
- I like evaded realities.

01:04:37.783 --> 01:04:40.300
I like creating worlds where the real,

01:04:40.300 --> 01:04:42.740
where the realities of the world

01:04:42.740 --> 01:04:45.653
in which we're living in
invades it and seeps into it,

01:04:47.140 --> 01:04:48.250
As well as doing the opposite,

01:04:48.250 --> 01:04:50.250
which is looking head on, as you said,

01:04:50.250 --> 01:04:51.552
with the Boko Haram stories.

01:04:51.552 --> 01:04:54.080
I like looking head on at things

01:04:54.080 --> 01:04:57.560
in a very steady, intense way

01:04:57.560 --> 01:05:01.153
but also like the indirect leaking in.

01:05:06.421 --> 01:05:09.190
- I have another question
more general about the book.

01:05:09.190 --> 01:05:10.870
It's a collection of short story

01:05:10.870 --> 01:05:13.590
that I know you've collected
for all your career.

01:05:13.590 --> 01:05:14.640
Sorry, yeah (laughs).

01:05:15.593 --> 01:05:16.426
I should stop.

01:05:16.426 --> 01:05:17.259
- I like it, I like it.

01:05:17.259 --> 01:05:18.873
We have to keep alternating.

01:05:18.873 --> 01:05:21.980
- (laughs) It's an alternate
reality, is I have something.

01:05:21.980 --> 01:05:23.770
Also speaking of alternate realities,

01:05:23.770 --> 01:05:27.160
I had a question because
this, in the lecture,

01:05:27.160 --> 01:05:30.080
the chapter, the part five is missing.

01:05:30.080 --> 01:05:32.390
And so I was wondering if it was like...

01:05:32.390 --> 01:05:33.223
In my head I was like,

01:05:33.223 --> 01:05:34.650
oh! Maybe it's like a chapter somewhere

01:05:34.650 --> 01:05:36.690
that is hidden somewhere in the book.

01:05:36.690 --> 01:05:38.020
And I just realized when you read it,

01:05:38.020 --> 01:05:40.070
I had this like realization
at the same time

01:05:40.070 --> 01:05:44.110
that inspector Draper was
unraveling the mystery.

01:05:44.110 --> 01:05:46.950
I was like, oh! I'm unraveling
the mystery of my copy.

01:05:46.950 --> 01:05:48.255
It just (indistinct).

01:05:48.255 --> 01:05:50.580
(all laughing)

01:05:50.580 --> 01:05:53.240
So yeah, in this collection of series,

01:05:53.240 --> 01:05:55.680
how did you pick and arrange the order?

01:05:55.680 --> 01:05:57.560
And I knew that short story

01:05:57.560 --> 01:06:01.623
I consider the hardest form
of literature to write.

01:06:02.944 --> 01:06:05.840
For you, what was the advantage
of using these formats?

01:06:07.420 --> 01:06:08.604
- You mean the short story format

01:06:08.604 --> 01:06:10.590
or the way in which it was arranged?

01:06:10.590 --> 01:06:11.423
- Yeah, yeah.

01:06:11.423 --> 01:06:15.710
Why did you pick to do a
collection of short stories

01:06:15.710 --> 01:06:17.760
and instead of maybe
you could have expanded

01:06:17.760 --> 01:06:20.030
some of the other story that you had

01:06:20.030 --> 01:06:22.340
into like a full-fledged novel?

01:06:22.340 --> 01:06:25.400
Like, what is the advantage
of having this format?

01:06:25.400 --> 01:06:27.390
- Oh! Florian, Florian,

01:06:27.390 --> 01:06:31.030
the whole point of the
truth of a short story...

01:06:32.930 --> 01:06:34.640
Well, a lot of writers tend to think

01:06:34.640 --> 01:06:39.640
that the short story is really
a frustrated short novel

01:06:42.180 --> 01:06:44.459
or rather a stunted novel

01:06:44.459 --> 01:06:47.420
or a novel that just didn't
get a chance to grow.

01:06:47.420 --> 01:06:51.413
Hadn't had its vitamins and its protein.

01:06:52.470 --> 01:06:55.680
No, that's not the case,
that's not the case at all.

01:06:55.680 --> 01:06:59.180
If a short story can
be expanded to a novel,

01:06:59.180 --> 01:07:01.693
then it never was a short
story in the first place.

01:07:02.740 --> 01:07:04.027
It's just that you didn't realize it.

01:07:04.027 --> 01:07:07.763
And we have to remember
that people like Joyce,

01:07:08.727 --> 01:07:13.727
"Ulysses" was meant to be
one of the last short stories

01:07:14.740 --> 01:07:17.330
in "Dubliners".

01:07:17.330 --> 01:07:22.330
And it just grew and it
was never a short story.

01:07:22.450 --> 01:07:26.133
You can't expand a short
story into a novel.

01:07:27.413 --> 01:07:32.413
That's the nature of the short story.

01:07:32.600 --> 01:07:34.202
It is completely...

01:07:34.202 --> 01:07:39.000
It has a complete integrity
of its compactness.

01:07:39.000 --> 01:07:42.380
The complete integrity of the fact

01:07:42.380 --> 01:07:45.932
that it cannot be made more than it is.

01:07:45.932 --> 01:07:48.690
It's perfect and complete length

01:07:48.690 --> 01:07:50.600
and it's not really about the length.

01:07:50.600 --> 01:07:54.170
It is about the tensions
contained within it.

01:07:54.170 --> 01:07:57.150
It's about actually,
forgive the phrase Florian,

01:07:57.150 --> 01:07:58.970
It really is about is quantum...

01:07:58.970 --> 01:08:03.567
Is quantum universe, the
inner spaces, the core,

01:08:05.560 --> 01:08:08.593
the electrons, the
things working inside it,

01:08:10.140 --> 01:08:12.370
the way they work and
play with one another

01:08:12.370 --> 01:08:16.562
in a tremendously secret
inner world dynamism.

01:08:16.562 --> 01:08:20.160
The short story is really a world.

01:08:20.160 --> 01:08:21.383
It's not a compressed world.

01:08:21.383 --> 01:08:23.793
It's a complete world.

01:08:26.322 --> 01:08:28.390
It might appear that
you've taken a slice of it

01:08:28.390 --> 01:08:32.620
but the laws that make it are complete.

01:08:32.620 --> 01:08:36.840
And I love the short story form as a...

01:08:36.840 --> 01:08:41.320
For me, it's one of the best
forms explore certain thoughts,

01:08:43.540 --> 01:08:45.810
experiments, certain
questions, certain challenges,

01:08:45.810 --> 01:08:48.303
certain themes, certain obsessions.

01:08:49.443 --> 01:08:51.080
It's more than a laser.

01:08:51.080 --> 01:08:54.570
It's more than a laser,
more than an x-ray.

01:08:54.570 --> 01:08:59.570
It's a new kind of a very
high powered super lens

01:09:04.290 --> 01:09:08.390
that looks really deep into
the nature of a problem

01:09:08.390 --> 01:09:12.926
and that is able to distill it into this.

01:09:12.926 --> 01:09:15.870
I think it's a miraculous form

01:09:15.870 --> 01:09:20.660
and that's why I've done so few of them

01:09:20.660 --> 01:09:25.140
because it takes such a long time to craft

01:09:27.210 --> 01:09:29.060
so that they have this extraordinary,

01:09:29.980 --> 01:09:32.283
strange living tension.

01:09:33.960 --> 01:09:35.910
- Okay, thank you very much.

01:09:37.220 --> 01:09:41.390
Maybe we can take one last question

01:09:41.390 --> 01:09:42.223
and then-

01:09:42.223 --> 01:09:44.590
- Oh! Be generous, Florian.

01:09:44.590 --> 01:09:45.902
- Oh! Okay (laughs).

01:09:45.902 --> 01:09:46.890
We'll see.

01:09:46.890 --> 01:09:48.730
You've heard Ben, if
you have more questions,

01:09:48.730 --> 01:09:49.960
feel free to add them-

01:09:49.960 --> 01:09:53.811
- Yeah, take two or three more.

01:09:53.811 --> 01:09:55.280
- (laughs) Will do.

01:09:55.280 --> 01:09:58.453
So we have someone who who's writing that,

01:09:59.980 --> 01:10:02.430
thank you, I was fascinated by your work

01:10:02.430 --> 01:10:05.340
and I am currently writing
my bachelor dissertation

01:10:05.340 --> 01:10:09.647
on the "Famish Roads" and Unjoki's novel,

01:10:09.647 --> 01:10:11.140
"Transparent City".

01:10:11.140 --> 01:10:14.610
I am wondering if you know
of the concept of Ubunthu

01:10:14.610 --> 01:10:18.800
and if you think that this
or commonly in general,

01:10:18.800 --> 01:10:21.500
is one of the steps that
leads to a better world

01:10:21.500 --> 01:10:22.333
or do you think

01:10:22.333 --> 01:10:26.030
that urbanization,
globalization and capitalism

01:10:26.030 --> 01:10:29.056
have a negative impact on communities?

01:10:29.056 --> 01:10:30.920
At least the small one,
such as the compounds

01:10:30.920 --> 01:10:33.913
and then obstruct potential
growth and experiences.

01:10:35.260 --> 01:10:36.093
- Oh my goodness!

01:10:36.093 --> 01:10:38.010
That question is as far
from the quantum universe

01:10:38.010 --> 01:10:39.465
as you can possibly get.

01:10:39.465 --> 01:10:41.920
But then at the same time, it isn't

01:10:41.920 --> 01:10:46.920
because I mean, Ubuntu
is in how the philosophy

01:10:50.410 --> 01:10:55.410
of a quantum universe affects
and shapes a whole world.

01:10:56.700 --> 01:10:59.700
Yeah, I think Ubuntu is a
very great African concept

01:10:59.700 --> 01:11:02.180
that can have very great
important consequences

01:11:03.204 --> 01:11:04.240
for the world.

01:11:04.240 --> 01:11:09.240
It's a philosophy that should
be made more widely known.

01:11:13.585 --> 01:11:17.127
It's a philosophy of
compassion, of community,

01:11:21.710 --> 01:11:26.710
of harmony and yeah, I
love the sound of it.

01:11:28.170 --> 01:11:30.340
And what was the question
about globalization?

01:11:30.340 --> 01:11:33.600
Has it had an impact on communities?

01:11:33.600 --> 01:11:34.680
- Yes.

01:11:34.680 --> 01:11:35.900
Especially the smaller ones.

01:11:35.900 --> 01:11:38.680
If you want, you can also open the panel.

01:11:38.680 --> 01:11:39.513
It's a long question then.

01:11:39.513 --> 01:11:41.194
So you should be able to see it.

01:11:41.194 --> 01:11:42.720
It's the first one on the top.

01:11:42.720 --> 01:11:43.750
- Yeah, I'm going to.

01:11:43.750 --> 01:11:45.164
I'm not going to get drawn into that.

01:11:45.164 --> 01:11:46.476
That would be too complicated for me.

01:11:46.476 --> 01:11:48.117
(all laughing)

01:11:48.117 --> 01:11:50.623
To get into the whole, yeah.

01:11:51.810 --> 01:11:53.320
To give a very short answer,

01:11:53.320 --> 01:11:56.010
yes, globalization has
had a negative impact

01:11:57.230 --> 01:11:59.793
on smaller communities.

01:12:00.890 --> 01:12:05.323
It's one of the difficult
things to deal with.

01:12:10.290 --> 01:12:13.860
How to keep the richness
of small communities

01:12:15.350 --> 01:12:19.203
within the impact of globalization.

01:12:21.030 --> 01:12:22.533
And yet at the same time,

01:12:23.440 --> 01:12:28.117
globalization is one of
the facts of our world.

01:12:30.857 --> 01:12:34.463
I'm a great believer in
not wishing away a fact.

01:12:34.463 --> 01:12:37.580
A fact is something we have to deal with,

01:12:37.580 --> 01:12:42.410
negotiate, fight, neutralize, work with,

01:12:42.410 --> 01:12:46.880
do something about, but
we have to face them.

01:12:46.880 --> 01:12:48.286
It's what we writers do.

01:12:48.286 --> 01:12:50.720
We're not good at running away from facts.

01:12:50.720 --> 01:12:55.533
We look at them and give them an image.

01:12:56.407 --> 01:12:57.940
And the impact of
globalization can be seen

01:12:57.940 --> 01:13:02.940
in quite a few of the
stories in this collection.

01:13:05.920 --> 01:13:06.753
- Thank you.

01:13:07.850 --> 01:13:11.300
On that topic of African beliefs,

01:13:11.300 --> 01:13:13.640
we have another question of like,

01:13:13.640 --> 01:13:17.410
how do the African spiritual beliefs

01:13:17.410 --> 01:13:20.167
with the fluidity
between the natural world

01:13:20.167 --> 01:13:23.090
and the spiritual realm
influence the story?

01:13:23.090 --> 01:13:24.363
Or do they?

01:13:27.370 --> 01:13:28.520
- I'm not sure that they do

01:13:28.520 --> 01:13:33.520
except to the degree that maybe
my mind is already saturated

01:13:34.010 --> 01:13:39.010
with certain beliefs that
I grew up with in Nigeria.

01:13:43.050 --> 01:13:43.883
Not just a belief,

01:13:43.883 --> 01:13:48.530
but this sense that the
boundaries between realms

01:13:48.530 --> 01:13:51.240
is more fluid than we think.

01:13:51.240 --> 01:13:55.710
Maybe that already predisposed
me to be interested

01:13:55.710 --> 01:14:00.710
in the quantum universe
where the invasion,

01:14:04.418 --> 01:14:09.418
where suddenly reality
is for the first time

01:14:10.330 --> 01:14:15.090
in a scientific context under great doubt

01:14:15.090 --> 01:14:18.543
and great scrutiny, great uncertainty.

01:14:20.106 --> 01:14:21.610
The other thing I want to add

01:14:21.610 --> 01:14:23.643
quite separate from that, Florian,

01:14:24.570 --> 01:14:28.040
about my fascination
with quantum mechanics

01:14:28.040 --> 01:14:32.540
is the place of
consciousness in all of this.

01:14:32.540 --> 01:14:34.190
And of course, this is something

01:14:34.190 --> 01:14:38.290
that quantum physicists are not...

01:14:38.290 --> 01:14:40.413
I don't know how you deal with it.

01:14:41.808 --> 01:14:44.210
You deal with the external aspect

01:14:44.210 --> 01:14:46.070
of the effect of measurement,

01:14:46.070 --> 01:14:51.070
the effect of measurement
on or the effect of looking,

01:14:52.060 --> 01:14:56.170
of witnessing, of observing
on the actual process,

01:14:56.170 --> 01:15:00.550
the split experiments, for example.

01:15:00.550 --> 01:15:05.543
But I am fascinated in what
it is about consciousness,

01:15:06.850 --> 01:15:09.540
about the conscious, the
fact of the consciousness

01:15:09.540 --> 01:15:14.540
of the observer that actually
gives rise to this effect,

01:15:14.570 --> 01:15:19.373
that actually gives rise to the
fixing of these subparticles

01:15:22.675 --> 01:15:25.670
into either a wave or a particle.

01:15:25.670 --> 01:15:30.670
I'm fascinated by this side
of the quantum equation, us

01:15:33.670 --> 01:15:35.990
and our consciousness and how it...

01:15:36.980 --> 01:15:40.930
What is going on here that
affects what's going on,

01:15:40.930 --> 01:15:42.393
if you can say, there?

01:15:44.045 --> 01:15:45.530
Can I ask you,

01:15:45.530 --> 01:15:47.925
is it something that is dealt with at all

01:15:47.925 --> 01:15:51.300
within the quantum institute?

01:15:51.300 --> 01:15:53.243
Is this something that you guys look at?

01:15:54.688 --> 01:15:56.260
- It's something like the observer effect,

01:15:56.260 --> 01:16:00.540
is something that of course
is really well-known here

01:16:00.540 --> 01:16:03.640
and then that they try to fight

01:16:03.640 --> 01:16:06.870
but I don't think there's a more like,

01:16:06.870 --> 01:16:10.260
there's a more philosophical
research of that

01:16:10.260 --> 01:16:11.093
like in the world

01:16:11.093 --> 01:16:13.550
because in the day-to-day
life of a researcher,

01:16:13.550 --> 01:16:14.750
they perform experiments

01:16:16.377 --> 01:16:19.620
and the way they deal
with this observer effect

01:16:19.620 --> 01:16:21.590
is that they use sights techniques

01:16:21.590 --> 01:16:26.029
to like measure indirectly
the measurements

01:16:26.029 --> 01:16:29.540
and perform calculation in an indirect way

01:16:29.540 --> 01:16:32.130
to avoid, per tube, the result

01:16:32.130 --> 01:16:34.900
and the inside of the experiments.

01:16:34.900 --> 01:16:38.260
And so I don't think
people are really thinking

01:16:38.260 --> 01:16:41.330
about how themself affect the results.

01:16:41.330 --> 01:16:44.130
I think they more are looking for ways

01:16:44.130 --> 01:16:46.510
to prevent that from happening

01:16:46.510 --> 01:16:49.630
and obtaining the right answer.

01:16:49.630 --> 01:16:52.148
However, that's something that
the Yellow Quantum Institute

01:16:52.148 --> 01:16:54.860
has been trying to investigate

01:16:54.860 --> 01:16:57.060
by hiring artists in residence.

01:16:57.060 --> 01:17:00.130
We had a few people coming in

01:17:00.130 --> 01:17:03.240
and trying to think about these topics

01:17:03.240 --> 01:17:07.610
and that's something that
I don't think a researcher,

01:17:07.610 --> 01:17:10.970
like who are trying to build
a quantum computer currently

01:17:10.970 --> 01:17:12.080
have time to think about that.

01:17:12.080 --> 01:17:14.580
There's a huge race in
the US and the world

01:17:14.580 --> 01:17:16.010
to build the first quantum computers

01:17:16.010 --> 01:17:20.340
and I think it's gonna be
another pan of the research

01:17:20.340 --> 01:17:24.810
or of maybe having people from
the humanities and philosophy

01:17:24.810 --> 01:17:26.420
trying to think about these topics.

01:17:26.420 --> 01:17:28.820
And I think bringing in artists

01:17:28.820 --> 01:17:33.010
that might be able to
use a different technique

01:17:33.010 --> 01:17:36.870
or different medium to
sort of open a reflection

01:17:36.870 --> 01:17:38.530
is something that we've done.

01:17:38.530 --> 01:17:42.030
For example, in this space
that's in front of me,

01:17:42.030 --> 01:17:45.260
we used to have a big quantum computer.

01:17:45.260 --> 01:17:48.480
And like, it was an art
piece done by Martha Lewis

01:17:48.480 --> 01:17:50.610
that had cameras and sensor.

01:17:50.610 --> 01:17:54.683
And when the visitor will
go inside this installation,

01:17:55.770 --> 01:17:58.270
the visitor was perturbing
the calculation that was done

01:17:58.270 --> 01:18:00.210
with lights and sensor.

01:18:00.210 --> 01:18:02.010
And so it was interesting to see

01:18:02.010 --> 01:18:05.780
that the art piece was working perfectly,

01:18:05.780 --> 01:18:08.910
performing a very simple
quantum computation

01:18:08.910 --> 01:18:10.917
but it was working without anybody in it.

01:18:10.917 --> 01:18:14.200
And as soon as a visitor enter the space,

01:18:14.200 --> 01:18:15.410
the camera will pick it up

01:18:15.410 --> 01:18:18.420
and then will sort of like
disturb the calculation

01:18:18.420 --> 01:18:21.520
and you couldn't really do
the calculation anymore.

01:18:21.520 --> 01:18:25.710
And so there was a way to see ourselves

01:18:25.710 --> 01:18:29.750
disturbing the quantum world in a sense.

01:18:29.750 --> 01:18:32.210
So of course, everything was at quantum

01:18:32.210 --> 01:18:35.250
because there was at a
scale, at the human scale

01:18:35.250 --> 01:18:37.610
and it was doing in classical computers.

01:18:37.610 --> 01:18:39.660
But we were trying to start

01:18:39.660 --> 01:18:43.910
and to create this tiny
idea in people's heads

01:18:43.910 --> 01:18:45.823
to start thinking about these topics.

01:18:46.860 --> 01:18:47.750
- Interesting.

01:18:47.750 --> 01:18:49.593
We have to have more
of these conversations.

01:18:50.786 --> 01:18:55.786
I think science needs
to take an interest...

01:19:00.650 --> 01:19:02.240
At some point in the future,

01:19:02.240 --> 01:19:05.470
we're gonna have to deal with
the science of consciousness.

01:19:05.470 --> 01:19:07.134
At some point.

01:19:07.134 --> 01:19:09.280
Maybe in 1,000 years time

01:19:09.280 --> 01:19:12.010
but we're gonna have to deal with it.

01:19:12.010 --> 01:19:14.610
- And if it's a bit shorter
timeframe than that,

01:19:14.610 --> 01:19:15.750
maybe there will be a good excuse

01:19:15.750 --> 01:19:17.692
to have you come to the YQI.

01:19:17.692 --> 01:19:19.550
(all laughing)

01:19:19.550 --> 01:19:22.725
But if it's far on you as well,
we'll have to wait on that.

01:19:22.725 --> 01:19:24.877
- I might still be around, who knows?

01:19:24.877 --> 01:19:26.400
(all laughing)

01:19:26.400 --> 01:19:29.360
We'll decompose time, slow time down.

01:19:29.360 --> 01:19:30.510
- That's better.

01:19:30.510 --> 01:19:32.700
- [Ben] Any other questions before I go?

01:19:32.700 --> 01:19:36.860
- Let me have a quick look in the chats.

01:19:36.860 --> 01:19:38.900
Yes, we have one last question

01:19:38.900 --> 01:19:41.340
which is on the topic of short story.

01:19:41.340 --> 01:19:45.240
Who do you admire as master of
the art from past or present?

01:19:45.240 --> 01:19:47.640
Is there any modern day practitioner

01:19:47.640 --> 01:19:49.420
that excites you on this?

01:19:49.420 --> 01:19:52.733
So it's a more general on
the theme of short story.

01:19:54.210 --> 01:19:55.233
- Oh my goodness!

01:19:56.760 --> 01:19:59.870
- I guess what they want is
like maybe some recommendation,

01:19:59.870 --> 01:20:02.347
some good read recommendation
on short story (laughs).

01:20:03.400 --> 01:20:07.040
- Yeah, I'm very old fashioned

01:20:08.685 --> 01:20:10.130
and I mean, I read a
lot of new short stories

01:20:10.130 --> 01:20:13.680
but I go back to the old ones

01:20:13.680 --> 01:20:17.870
for the reasons I mentioned earlier

01:20:18.810 --> 01:20:21.010
because the thing about
a great short story,

01:20:22.582 --> 01:20:23.430
the thing about the great novel,

01:20:23.430 --> 01:20:26.180
the thing about a great poem,
a great piece of writing,

01:20:27.650 --> 01:20:31.433
is that it multiplies its realities.

01:20:32.290 --> 01:20:34.170
It really does.

01:20:34.170 --> 01:20:37.473
Not only is it not the same
every time you read it,

01:20:38.410 --> 01:20:39.800
it gets incredibly richer.

01:20:39.800 --> 01:20:41.410
It gets immeasurably richer.

01:20:41.410 --> 01:20:45.500
And I think we bring that
constant richness to it.

01:20:45.500 --> 01:20:47.090
But in order for that to happen,

01:20:47.090 --> 01:20:49.370
there is one condition for that to happen

01:20:49.370 --> 01:20:54.370
which is that the piece
itself has to have acquired,

01:20:55.250 --> 01:20:57.510
have had to have begun, first of all,

01:20:57.510 --> 01:21:02.170
with the condition of a very
high level of achievement,

01:21:02.170 --> 01:21:05.490
of accomplishment, of compression,
of truth, of integrity.

01:21:05.490 --> 01:21:07.620
Call it greatness, call
or whatever you want.

01:21:07.620 --> 01:21:09.810
It has to have had that first

01:21:09.810 --> 01:21:14.350
and then that has to
have interacted and moved

01:21:14.350 --> 01:21:17.040
and played and danced and
be shaped and shaped in

01:21:18.673 --> 01:21:21.480
and time itself and
consciousness and many readers

01:21:21.480 --> 01:21:23.810
and so a text is enriched

01:21:23.810 --> 01:21:27.560
by the vast number of secret
readings brought to it

01:21:29.700 --> 01:21:32.320
or to them.

01:21:32.320 --> 01:21:36.190
And so I tend to go back to
the reading enriched texts,

01:21:39.390 --> 01:21:42.210
texts that have played and
worked and dreamt through time.

01:21:42.210 --> 01:21:45.080
So I love some of the
Savantas short stories

01:21:47.581 --> 01:21:49.330
and here's another very
interesting one, "Donkey Shooter",

01:21:49.330 --> 01:21:51.520
a book that's almost 1,000 pages long.

01:21:51.520 --> 01:21:54.660
It began as a short story,
that tells you something

01:21:54.660 --> 01:21:58.960
about the way in which the
short can contain the vast

01:22:00.254 --> 01:22:02.963
and the vast can contain the small.

01:22:04.035 --> 01:22:06.380
I check over, I keep going back

01:22:06.380 --> 01:22:11.380
to the many wonderful modern masters.

01:22:14.030 --> 01:22:16.360
Many of them can be read in
the pages of the "New York"

01:22:16.360 --> 01:22:19.990
and magazines and
anthologists around the world.

01:22:19.990 --> 01:22:24.990
There are many young and
excellent African writers.

01:22:26.750 --> 01:22:29.400
It's not hard to find really,
really good short stories

01:22:29.400 --> 01:22:32.833
but if you want to deep
travel on a deep journey,

01:22:33.870 --> 01:22:35.740
well, you could go to the ones

01:22:35.740 --> 01:22:39.663
that have acquired their presence in time.

01:22:44.070 --> 01:22:46.830
Go to people like Chekov, yes.

01:22:46.830 --> 01:22:48.800
More personal, yeah.

01:22:48.800 --> 01:22:50.310
- Thank you so much, Ben.

01:22:50.310 --> 01:22:51.960
Maybe we can conclude on that

01:22:51.960 --> 01:22:54.280
by doing some entanglement experiments.

01:22:54.280 --> 01:22:56.270
When I can show the UK
version of your book

01:22:56.270 --> 01:22:59.097
and you can show us the
US version of your book.

01:23:00.940 --> 01:23:05.710
So, yes, the link to buy the
book is somewhere in the chat,

01:23:05.710 --> 01:23:07.770
you should be able to find it.

01:23:07.770 --> 01:23:09.690
Thank you so much for
the time that you have

01:23:09.690 --> 01:23:11.880
to read this wonderful short story.

01:23:11.880 --> 01:23:13.160
Thank you for answering all your questions

01:23:13.160 --> 01:23:15.460
and thank you all for attending.

01:23:15.460 --> 01:23:16.880
- Thank you all very much.

01:23:16.880 --> 01:23:18.540
It's been a real pleasure.

01:23:18.540 --> 01:23:20.670
I hope we meet in the flesh someday

01:23:20.670 --> 01:23:22.330
and look after yourself, thank you.

01:23:22.330 --> 01:23:23.770
- That would be with great pleasure.

01:23:23.770 --> 01:23:24.640
Thank you very much, Ben.

01:23:24.640 --> 01:23:25.940
Have a good night.

01:23:25.940 --> 01:23:27.540
- Florian, thank you.

01:23:27.540 --> 01:23:28.740
Hope we meet soon.

01:23:28.740 --> 01:23:29.573
Take care.

